        <head>LIV b RPOOL</head>
        <p>LIV b RPOOL</p>
        <p>yOUR ADVERTISENIKNT in the "Fxaminet. plates it before throw whc)m yen want to reaohand goes direct to tbe Pun/ie, keeps your name before theme serves to make you etbcr Known among talent. at a very small o,ost. eOMMRECIAL, PRINTING EXECUTED AT IHE LowEsT PRICE. WITH THE GREA Es DE 'PATCH.</p>
        <p>_ PROPRO.TORB OF THE WARRINGTON AND MI D.O .IE4'IIHE EXIMINER. EA RLESTOWN AND IN EW lON EXAMINER, RUNCORN EXAMINER. WIDNES EXAMINER ST. HELENS EXAMINER: TXT BEWSEY STREET. WV A RRI YGTON. MAMMOTH POSTERS. A SPECIALITY: MAMMOTH POSTEES. LARGE TYPE MAMMOTH POSTE:OS. FOR MAMMOTH POSTRRS. THE AIOUT STUMM* ML 451 an POSTE S. PirsT ft MAMMOTH POSTE ES. Produced l be Country. MAMMOTH POSTERS. MAMMOTH PoSTIMS. A SP- otALITY. POSFLRS OF ALL SIZES May be ineptct• d &amp;Rd Ittiinatea obtained at the ffice, JOHN W ALKER AND C</p>
        <p>2 M 0 DOUG AIL'S PATBZIT SELF-RAISING FLOUR 93011Trity.</p>
        <p>'LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>Ifi Dna NELL/E.</p>
        <p>There is some charming millinery procurable la town just now; indeed, one finds it difficult to choose where all is so attractive. A particularly effective specimen was alarge hat of black crinoline, fancifully bent, and decorated with mauve and 'white lilac and just a touch of black tulle. A pretty toque was of tucked mauve tulle, with black quills, and the left side slightly raised by a twisted bandeau of black tulle. Another toque was of tucked Tuscan-coloured tulle, with three or four mauve poppies round the right side of the brim, ands, bow of mauve panne ribbon and osprey on the left. By the way, I am informed that there barn been a great run of late on black crinoline hats —a somewhat surprising piece of information.</p>
        <p>Girls of all ages under 12 are wearing long pelisses and paletots. Some of the most attractive paletets are of fine face-cloth in pastel-blue, pink, or green, with a very large tippet-like collar, of white satin, edged with either ribbon-applique or heavy guipure. This collar wraps over to the left aide of the chest, and fastens there under a large ribbon rosette, the small V-shaped opening left at the neck being filled in with a little vest matching the cape-collar. Another pretty type of long coat is of old-rose cloth strapped with the same and stitched with black, the bodice part being double- breasted and fastening with large black buttons. The skirts of the majority of the paletots have the skirt-part arranged so that it hangs in pleats, and the sleeves are rather wide, with somewhat elaborate cuffs. There is, however, another modish shape which is cut en Printesse, the skirt widening out umbrella-fashion towards the hem, without pleats, and the upper part being finished with triple capes of spotted panne, or of the much. favoured embroidered linen.</p>
        <p>For a delightful summer gown silk warp taffeta is one of the cheapest and daintiest materials for summer wear. It ig soft, silky and of fine warp, "much thicker than voile, and not transparent, and some of the colourings are simply charming. The blues are undoubtedly too bright, but the greys, fawns, and dove tints are exquisite. Another harming material is the silk spot crêpe de Chine. This material is semi-transparent, hut looks well over sateen or moirette, although silk is prefer- able. The palest fawn, which is almost it biscuit tint, woold make up delightfully. Ecru gipure er roie point in a hold pattern is admirable for the trimmings, and the imitations of these laces are quite moderate in price, and really handsome and deceptive.</p>
        <p>The heat and moisture of the summer moutits have a tendency to rust metals, mildew fabrics amid cover all sorts of substances with mould. Fames- Utica and putrefaction develop rapidly in vege- table and animal substances if they are not oars- fully watched. Lime and charcoal are two aide toward keeping the house sweet and dry, and the housekeeper should, if possible, provide herself with both of these materials. A barrel each of lime and charcoal in the cellar will tend to keep that part of the house dry and sweet. A bowl of lime in a damp closet will dry and sweeten it. A dish of charcoal in a closet or refrigerator will do much toward making these places sweet. The power of charcoal to absorb odours is much greater directly after it has been burned than when it has been exposed to the air for a length of time. Charcoal may be purified and used again and again by heath's it to a red heat. The lime must be kept in a place where there is no chance of its gutting wet. Your loving JaNNIN.</p>
        <p>Coax SOLES FOE. llooTs.—Cork, being a non- conductor of heat, is an excellent preservative of warmth. Cork soles should be placed inside of shoes or boots, but see that by adding them you do not make your boots too tight, or the circula- tion will be impeded. A free circulation is essen- tial. The cork being light, no inconvenience will be felt from the extra weight. THE HANDS. —lmperfect washing and incom- plete drying of the hands, especially in cold weather, renders them rough, red and liable to chap. To dabble the hands hastily and frequently In hard water of varying temperature, to neglect to rinse off the soap, and to dry them carelessly on ft rough, unabsorbent towel, is to invite a most u.:- •eautiful condition of the hands.</p>
