        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The pensions given to various ladies of Queen Vic- , toria's Court, are understood to be on a more limited fcalo than was expected. Unlike our late Sovereign,the 'King has no private fortune, and the pensions seem to have shared in the various other economies which at to be effected in the Royal Hcusetiolds. The K,ng has to keep up the two private residences of Balmoral, and Osborne, for which our late Queen had no difficulty in providing out of her private means, if necesaary. 13117. the King, as Sir Michad Hicks-Beach I said iu the House of Common?, has nothing m‘ire than the grant from Parliament, and it is certain that Bal- moral and Osborne do not cost less than £30,000 or £40,000 a year to keep up. Theret,re though the (jail List has been increased, it is probable that the King, with his xperp•es at Sandringham. has really lesa to do I • k on the whole, than our late Sovereign.</p>
        <p>Mr Max O'Rell has all sera in advice' to offer bctk sexes in his latest book, "Her Reyal Highness, Woman," which has just been issued by Mresrs Chatto, and Windus. This oistinguished author looks upon the world from the humorous side if things, but always with a shrewd intuition into the realities of human nature, which makes him one of the most engaging writers. His books OD the manners and customs in different climes are full of good things, and so also is his latest work, which dials more extensively with woman as she is in ill nationalities, and in all her various spheres and aspirations in life. It is interesting as are all Mr Max O'Rell's bocks, and it has that peculiar merit of combining fun with phi rosopy without being vulgar. Mr MAX O'Rell tells us what he c-assiders the ideal woman, but except that he gives the palm to the American variety, be Ones not tell us where she is to be found, Cr how we are to knew her when we see her. Ii is much the same with the counsel which the " Lancet" offers us this week,and wh ch tells us bat we all should laugh more. But w howl uld not laugh who could, ex- cept perhaps the few who are unduly nervous of growing fat With many of us it is the Wiry sett's: than ti e desire to laugh that we want, for, as the " Lancet" says, those who laugh keep their health and their frierds, while the aloe° e not only shorten their dsys, but occasion few regrste when they take their departure. The young ean generally manage to do their share of the laughing, but to those who have no longer the enthusiasm of youth, and whose mirthfulness seldom rises above a half-smile, the " Lancet " strongly urges the advice that somehow or oiler they should spare time to laurel. We have long had fashions in hand6hakings and now lit seems that an attempts to be made to induce the I fashionable smile. It does not do to believe all one reads about the fads and fancies which Society and more I especially Society's tradespeople, are ever seeking after, bu: it EDAM@ to be true that we are to have the cul- tivated smile amongst what is known as the" smart set," and which no doubt will be quickly imrtated by lest distinguished folk. It would not however, be alto- gether new, as acme years ago there was a society smile in New York, known as the Madison Cquare smile. That might beast deseribed'es a pai tug f ttlr lips w t a somewhat vacant expres,ion in the eve% and iedeed it may be easily understood thal a purely art II smile must make us look more or lets stupid. Speaking of New York, a young doctor in this part of the world, if we may believe the report, bas hit upon a novel plan of arriving at wealth and matrimony, and it is singular that the idea has not occurred to someone ' before Being desirous of starring practice in style. this embryo medico proposes to rafflehimsell fro. 3,000 (lasts at a pound each. No particulars are given as to hew the contest is to be decided, but be says he will marry the winner, cod share the money with her. Negresses, invalids, and women over thirty are not eligible, but other drawbacks such As supreme ugliness, will not die. qualify. It remains to be sen what nil come of this veaturesome proposal. If the laws permit it is quite conceivable that the wbole thing might tes carri,-d out in America, but in this country it is certain that the authorities would put a stop upon any such proceed- Inge, Paris fashions are by no means so valuable to British women as formerly. Our island taste has been educated and improved by long study of our neighbours across the Channel, whose notions reach us also in a round- about way, via, N.w York, where they are submitted to modifications, making them more suited to our Idea.. Stage modistes too seek the best models frcm the cities named aboveand frog? Berlin, and Vienna, and our fashion' are tbe result a this blend. Parisians now copy from us. Many of our materials are in every way superior to French fabrics, whilles our tailor-made dresses are imitated everywhere. Elaboizate drew'. sueh as we see depicted in fashion journals, on dames of im- possible figure and vacant feature, are, when dating- from Paris designed there just for the Baglish to copy. If an approximation to such extravagant styles were worn at all it would not be in the streets, but at indoor festivities only. newest silks are positive triumphs in weaving. Brocades, heavy and soft, ahd elacrki, are rrmt used,and the manntacturers seem to have tried bow much they can put into them. The background is as shot as are the colours on a dove's neck, or in a shell, pearly pink. and green, and heliotrope, with other delicate hues,often mastered by glowing copper or bronze, or in ennther light, by gray as fair as silver. This ground work is to be seen traversed by narrow velvet like lines of other changing hues, darker in scope, while a blurred, yet quite evident design of fir', Cr. and leaves is thrown moor the whole qui.:e distinct from the ground wink, and trona the stripes upot. it. Other new silks, leas elaborate, show quaint little markings, and these also are them- selves of chaeging tun e, and sql a background that varies chameleon like, according to environment. ether silks show is it were forked lightning run and of strange tint, upon black, or some strongly con- I treating _lane.</p>
        <p>Yet 0 wild,</p>
        <p>„ Then there are the silks, woven in bandana colours, and intermixed with gold thresos. The: designs some- times. but not always, sugentst these homely bandker. chiefs. ether gorgeous textiles are silk and cotton muslin'. white or coloured, and also stiff and shining svi,h gold and silver threars. These have a ticcorative Value apart from their proper nee for dress or for dress accessories. rhe gaudy silks flung over sofa and settee. thrown over the piano. or dropp.ug from mantel-piece and soreen, brighten up a dingy drawing room wonder- fully. The pretty muslin. serve a similar purpose and appear over cushions and headrests, while wisely chosen lengths of silk and muslin cover the tea-table, or relieve without concealing heaps of music pages or of magazines .Their value depends upon the fusee-re-that arrange them. Draperies—lately so-callea—linger only in dingy lodg- ings, but gaudy scarves, and snowy and glittering' muslin, carefully used, and occasionally renewed, have an almolt Eastern brightness, and revive vague memories of the Arabian Nigbts. _</p>
        <p>The present popularity for lace is no doubt due to recent hot summers, a repetition of which wilt not find us so 4.npri pared as formerly. It is. tc the expected heat that we owe our short and transparent sleeves, while neck bands are to be equally open. rile high fmicem of buckram are disraced in favor of a mere shallow band often of lace, and threaded with baby ribbons. Larger and turn-over collars, and yokes, are again of openwork, though sellen too great an expanse is displayed the lace is backed witb thin silk or chiffon. " MADGE."</p>
