      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>LONDON MISSIONARY SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>LONDON MISSIONARY SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Sir Charles Elliott, K.O 8.1., presided at the Annual Public Meeting and G. eerall Meet.og of members of this iciety, held od Mends) evening at the City Temple there was a rrwded attendance. The Rev. E. Wardlaw Thompson (Foreign Secretary) read an abstract of the Annual Report, which was really a general review of the w. rk done during the previous decade. With re- gard to the F award Movement inaugurated in 1881,end by which it waa n•oposed to anticipate the Society's Centenary in 1895 by sending out 100 additional workers to reinforce the steff, the result hue been that the num- of European intssiunaries has been increased during the decade from 198 tt 270. By far the larger part 01 ' the increased Ektaff Sets ec,ne to meet the needs of the Great Easeern MifalOW3 in India and China. Tba number of medical missionaries has been mire than doubled—seven of the twenty-eight now at work being ladies, of whern two are wives of missionarige ; anti four ladies who are trained nurses are superintending the nursing arrangement and training native Chustian women in the Miseien hospitals. The Watcher,' b, rid commenced in 1892 to unite together in one fellowship of prater all the friends of the London Missionary Society at borne and abroad, has steadily spread, and now numbers 28,000 memhers. In various parts of he Missioc-fi, Id increasing attention Is being paid to the very important duty of pelf-help, as in the !churches of the South Sass, in Central and South China, amongst the Malagasy Christians, and even among the very poer who constitute ne great nisj; fit), of the Christian community in Ir dia. The total income for the pa., ye., sineun'ed to £141.163 to meet an exper diture ot £l5O 886. It was pousted out that the society's difficulty as to finance is not due to the decrease of con- tributions, but to the increase of expenditure; and that this increase of expenditure is not due to launching mit upon any new mission, but entirely to the natural un- avoicable, and constantly-zo-be•expected growth of refponsibility and need in the districts which are already under the society's care. _ .</p>
        <p>Inter( s' tag addressee, setting forth, on the one hand the immense needs and difficulties of the work, and on the other the great encouragement which it affords,wrre delivered by the chairman, and by Dr T V Campbell (Jammulamadugw), the Rev J L Barton, D.D (Boston), the Rev D S Murray (North China), and others</p>
        <p>One of the most common and dangerous forms of Kidney Disease is</p>
