      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>DROPSY.</head>
        <p>DROPSY.</p>
        <p>The only way to cure Dropsy is to remove the cause, and in ordt to accomplish this thee Kidneys must be in perfect I?ealth. It the Kidneys be in condition to purify the blood, thoryoglily filtering the watery particles out of it, Dropsy iinVirimpibility: Restore the Kidneys to hea1:11 by using the only certe.in cure—</p>
        <p>ry:</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 17, 1901</p>
        <p>tliat she would do even that again and again If he had told her to do so. for he exercised a strange) h-pnotic influence over her. She despised herself for beinm the tool she was in his hands, yet she loved him to distraction. Three months ar she had not known him. How commonplace was life then And now, the excitement, the glamour of this secret affection.</p>
        <p>Douglas Low did not wait until he reached home before reading his letter. The roads were lonely, hut the parish control extended nearly to the Chase, and by the uncertain light of an oil lamp he pulled out Martin's letter, tore open the envelope, and devoured the contents.</p>
        <p>The letter was written upon Government-stamped parer, and somebody else had transcribed. the pr;soner's name on the top of the page, together with his number, and the date of the letter ; whilst the back of the ruled paper contained various regulations concerning any reply 4 I,rl might be made. "Useless regulations in this case," muttered Low, evilly, to himself. "Ife will have to wait till Doomsday for his answer."</p>
        <p>Ile read the letter through and clenched his teeth at the assurances of love which it contained. His innocence would yet be proved, Martin wrote, and be prayed that his lover might continue to believe him not guilty of the fearful crime ascribed to bini; and that she would continue to be true to him until, in God's time, the prison gates would be open and he would walk out a free man. Of his life in the prison he could not speak, but he abjured her not to discontinue her exertions to disprove his guilt. There were those who had given evidence against him at the trial who must hold the real secret.</p>
        <p>"The Parrs, of course," said Low. "And they will continue to hold it. The very fiend himself must he on my side. Thanks to that little fool at the post-office, this stroke of luck has come my way."</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIV. THE CUNNING OF THE CRAFT.</p>
        <p>He had not entirely lied when he told Kitty Lane that he had legal business to transact. The next evening a sinister, wizened-faced old man drove over from Market Fenhorough and dine i with him.</p>
        <p>"And now for business," said Low, when, the meal finished, they had repaired to his private room.</p>
        <p>"At your service, Mr. Low. Any more corners dif the estate to be mortgaged ? " p This reference to the way in which Low had been shuffling with the estate left to his steward- ship in order to raise private funds did not please the host of the evening. A frown creased itself on his forehead.</p>
        <p>"No," he said ; "but something that will pay you equally well. I have no doubt. The fact is, I want to do a good turn to a friend. I want a loan of two thousand pounds." The old lawyer affected no surprise, but merely asked : "And what security ?" "Ali, yes, security. I thought that was what you were going to ask. You lawyers wouldn't do your own mother a good turn without security for it. Well, you shall have good security this time. Come w h me."</p>
        <p>Ire took him up into the large and old-fashioned dining-hall, a relic of the older Chase, which had been partly demolished to make place for the more modern building. This old hall was crammed with valuable relics of a once wealthy and aristocratic family. The ancient Penwicks had once moved in the best society ; but during the last two or three generations the family had gone downhill. "There, what do you say to that Rubens ? It is worth at least three thousand pounds. In fact, I had that amount offered for it the other day only I know Martin was particularly fond of the picture and would not like it to leave the family." But old Lawyer Pryke was too cunning and too sagacious to close a bargain so readily. "I here are lots of people who don't care a rap for pictures," he said. "The value of a Rubens is only a sentimental matter, to my mind. Give me a more modern painting, something with the blue sea and ships, and so forth, or a bit of hunting, to decorate my walls with, and most people are the same. I shall want better security than that before I can undertake to bring forward a client." "Well, take that little Correggio ; that's worth at least another thousand. His works are very rare. Any lover of art would rave for a week over a gem like that. I've heard my cousin declaim about it for half-an-hour at a stretch."</p>
        <p>put old Pryke shewed no particular interest in the pictures. "I should want half a dozen of such," he said.</p>
        <p>"Oh, hang it then, take them!" replied Low. "Pick out what you want, and set your own value upon them. They are doing no giPod there, and I hate the very sight of the things. Pictures and music are my two detestations." "Is this place insured to a sufficient amount to cover the pictures ?"</p>
        <p>"I can't tell you off-hand," said Lou. "Very likely it 1,. I should think it most probable, for both my uncle and my cousin were cranks over things like these. But I can assure you I shan't trouble to pay heavy insurance rates for the things when the time for renewing comes. So if you like to have them removed—that's what is in your mind, I suppose—you can, and pay the insurai cc rates yourself." "I know a friend in town," said Old Pryke, "who would be glad to store them for us. And he would insure them too, just for the pleasure of having them fu his house." "Very well, I'm agreeable." The old lawyer chuckled at his own astuteness, for the loan of the pictures to the pseudo art fancier and critic would bring him in a matter of a hundred a year. Lawyer Pryke knew how to arrange these little things to a nicety. He was very careful in his choice of the pictures which were to act as security for the money. This he proposed to borrow himself at three-and-a-half per cent., and then relend at 'four-and-a-half. When his selection had been made, the auction price of Martin Fenwick's property, whioh these men were dealing with, would not have been lase than six or seven thousand pounds. "And now," said Low, when they had settled the business and returned to his private room, "there is another matter I want to see you about. I may as well discuss it while you are over." The other was all attention. Ile was gradually getting his skinny fist on piece after piece of the Chase property, for which he had lotig acted as lawyer. What was coming next? It would be grist to his mill,. he doubted not,. whatever .it was.</p>
        <p>" "This matter is hardly my business. It is more of the nature of a personal favour to me," began Low.</p>
        <p>Old Pryke did not feel so keenly interested. "I'm afraid I can't stop long," he said, pulling out his watch. "I see it is late. Perhaps you would ask your man to put in my horse." "There is no hurry," said Low. "I want. you to do this as a great personal favour, and, above everything, to keep it quiet. You, I believe, hold the mortgage securities on the Fenwick Barton?" "Yes ; but they are not in my name." "Well, don't you think they have been held long enough. Wouldn't it be to your interest to foreclose ? There would be a new mortgage, you know, and you lawyers always come in for some pickings over a little matter like that." " Humph ! Perhaps so. Believe me, I should have foreclosed long ago, but what was the good? The old Barton is scarcely worth the trouble. What would happen,. do you think? You ought to know." "I think I do."</p>
        <p>"Well, it would be this, especially just now after that fire. Old Hendon would cry: Iree,cavi,' throw up the sponge, and let you romp in and do what you like with the place. And what's the good of that to me? Put up for auction at the present state of the market, the buildings and the goodwill of the farm wouldn't fetch half enough to repay me the advance I made. No, Low, that was the worst., the most unprofitable thing I ever launched in ; but now it is bad, I may as well go on picking up my interest for another ten years. The farm will be no poorer in the market then than now—that's impossible. If it has to go under the hammer, I may- as 'well wait and get a little more out of it" "You can get everything out of it, if you go to work my way," said Low. "Wait for a month, and then send in your notice to foreclose." "And what wonderful thing is going to happen then?"</p>
        <p>"Simply this. That two thousand pounds you are advancing me goes to Hendon." "Oh, the deuce it does. And ho pays the interest, I suppose, you being responsible," said Pryle, greatly interested, for lie could see a peculiar dodge in the offering, and he always pricked up his ears when trickery was afoot, even though he were not the master trickster in the scheme of affairs. "Yes, he pays the interest to me. I have secured him this loan from a friend to tide him over. It is barely sufficient to 'lear him from the heavy liabilities involved by the fire, But that is nothing to do with you. All you have to do is to forward that cheque within a couple of days; and, ' twless you hear from we to the contrary, Pend in</p>
        <p>your notice to foreclose, and make all you like out of the transaction." When Lawyer Pryke left that night, wondering at the reason for the deep game his client was playing, Low leant back in his chair and chuckled deep and long. He wag playing a deep game, and, he assured himself, playing it well.</p>
      </div>
