        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Funeral of the Late Mr. Isaac Houghton. ' The remains of the late Mr base Houghton, of Mersey-street, and late of Woolston whose death occurred last week were interred in the Cemetery on Monday afternoon. The officiating clergyman was the Rector, the Rev Canon Willis, who con- ducted the services both in the church and at the graveside. The mourners present were ;---Mesers Joseph It Houghton, F Houghton and James R Houghton (sons). J Houghton. Thomas Houghton, James Houghton and W Ii Houghton (brother.). Joseph H Houghton, Herbert A Bennett 1,111 A Wright (nephews) and Mr Joseph Wright (brother- in-law). A large number of beautiful floral tributes had been received. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr J Mounfleld.</p>
        <p>A Leigh Man's Singular Death. On Monday the Wigan police reported the death of James Rhodes (3'7), a plasterer, of Lingwrond- street, Bedford Leigh. Deceased was emploved by a Liverpool firm of plasterers, and was engaged at the Municipal Buildings. Warrington. when he complained of having strained hissaelf. He was taken home to Leigh, where his removal was (order- ed to the Wigan Infirmary on Saturday. He died, however, on Sunday. Sequel to .the Cyclist Manceuvres.—The Man</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Who Wouldn't.</head>
        <p>Who Wouldn't.</p>
        <p>The Beater cycling manoluvres of the Slaircheatee VolunPeers have Cost Joseph Wardle about £4. Be is a member of the Swintcn cycling detachment of the Ist V.B.H.R During the Halter Manceuvres he wee one of a section ordered to proceed from Ashton to Warrington. but the weather was bad, and at Hatley be refused to obey orders, used offensive language to the sergeant, and rode off home. He was dismissed_frem the corps and ordered to pay £3 15s,tbe amount of the grant which the corps hae lost by his inefficiency. Such was the statement of the adjutant to the Ecclem Bench on Monday. Wardle in excuse said he reached Gatley without food or money to get any. He was promised 2s on reaching Warrington, but that would not have Seen sufficient to pay for his food and lodging. Toe Adjutant : The other men manlged to scrape along somehow.—The Chairman. Aldermen 6pary (captain of the ht Andrew's Boys' Brigade), lectured the defendant on the value of discipline. He mu..t obey, whether be bed anything to .at or not, and place his trust in the authorities. Even if he was told to do whit seemed impossible, he muse., "have a try at it."—An order was made for Wardle to pay £3 15s and costs. Day Schools Re-opened. With the exception of Christ Churc"‘ Latchford, all the schools of the borbugh which had been closed owing to an outbreak of measles. were re-opened on Monday worming. The epidemic iirapidly declining. _ _ _</p>
        <p>1 WaS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>(Copyrigil.j</head>
        <p>(Copyrigil.j</p>
        <p>THE LADDER OP GUILT.</p>
        <p>BY lILYSS ROGF:RS, Author of "For Love and the Queen," "Wedded</p>
        <p>in Mystkry, " &amp;c.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECI D,NG CHAPTERS: Douglas Low, madly in love with Grace Hendon, daughter of a neighbouring farmer, attempts to murder his cousin, --Martin Fenwick. His aim, however, un- accountably fails, and Squire Fenwick, his uncle, is the victim instead. The deed was witnessed by an old bag who has a grudge against the Fenwicks, and who accuses htertin of the murder. Martin is tried and found guilty. The execution fails, and a second attempt a week later is also abortive. Martin Fenwick is reprieved, and removed to a convict prison. Meanwhile, Douglas Low meditates new villainies. ' At his prompting Farmer Hendon's granaries are fired. Farmer Hendon fails, and Douglas Low presses for his daughter's hand.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIII.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>SNARE OP TUE FOWLER.</head>
        <p>THE SNARE OP TUE FOWLER.</p>
        <p>A week had elapsed since Douglas Low's visit to - Fenwick Barton, and he had not yet renewed the coll. To Farmer Hendon's mind this was an act of kind forbearance, an indication that Low did not wish to press unduly for an answer to the request he had proffered; and the old farmer, whatever he might lose by it, had not the remotest intention of according the young man the faintest hope that he might ever win his daughter. Yet he could not help respecting Low the more for not forcing the position at a time when an unscrupulous man might have been expected to bring preffloure to bear.</p>
        <p>But how little did he know the man ! The surest, the deadliest blow is often the longest delayed. Douglas Low was exercising the saving grace of restraint only that he might play his evil game with the greater certainty. Even whilst the farmer, seated in his chair before the wide chimney corner, was mentally extolling him, Douglas Low was playing his ultimate card In the game in which he held the lead. The scene was the Chase Copse, a dark-wooded hollow half a mile to the rear of the house. Time, nine in the evening. Douglas Low sat on a stile at the bottom of the hollow humming lowly to himself.</p>
        <p>Presently the sound of crackling twigs and leaves broke in upon his humming. A light footstep approached, and down the narrow path came a rustling of skirts. A woman appeared in view. Low stepped out into the path to meet her. He. took her in his arms and kissed her.</p>
        <p>"Am I late, Douglas? lam sorry to have kept you. It is so good of you to wait. I was afraid I should not have.been able to get away at, all to- night. Old Henderson was so awfully cross and the letters were late."</p>
        <p>"Has it come ye-C? is there anything for her ? " The woman still in his arms looked,archly up into his face. She was not pretty, but there was a winsome grace about her that was far more capti- vating than finely-chiselled features. "Why are you so anxious about it, Douglas? Really, I begin to feel quite jealous. Do you know I was told in the village yesterday that you were in love with this Grace Hendon, and wanted her before she was engaged to your cousin." Low forced a laugh. "They say very absurd things in the village, sweetheart," he said. "No, it is merely.a business matter. There are business secrets connected with the Chase that only my cousin knows, and it is of the utmost importance to me that I should become acquainted with them at whatever cost. He is certain to communicate some hint of them to his fiancee, if you can still fall her that, in the first letter he writes from Minewood. You will not fail me in this. It will mean much to me—to you, my dearest, to us both, when we come at length into the property." The girl's head was reposing trustfully upon Low's shoulder. She looked up saucily at him for a moment, and then drew something out of her pocket. It was a letter. "Look," she said, "it has come at last, only I wanted to hear from your own lips, Douglas, that there was no love between you and Grace Hendon. If I thought that, it would break my heart" But he was paying little heed to her words. The letter was in his hands. For months past be had been playing the game of love with this village post-office assistant in order that when at length Martin Fenwick could avail himself of the precious permission to write to his friends the letter lie would assuredly send to Grace might not fall into her hands. "Well, you haven't Ithanked me for it yet."</p>
        <p>, Low responded with a kiss. ‘.‘lf it should be found- out ? " she said. "0 Douglas, if—" "But it never will be found out, darling. Only you and I know of• its arrival, and the prison authorities are not likely to write to make any Inquiries." "Douglas," she said, "you won't mind what I've done, will you, but this afternoon--" She hesitated. _ _</p>
        <p>"Goon," he said. "I don't suppose it's any- thing: I'm likely to trouble over." "Well, this afterneon, when writing to my mother, I told her what you said last night." "The deuoe yoU did! Didn't that I ex retis p ly wanted the whole thing kept quiet ? " "I did not think, that there could be any harm in telling my mother of my'engagement: She will be so delighted. You did not say that / was not to mention it to my own folks." "Not in so maliy.words." • "And thefire` so Interested in all I do at home, Douglas. ••••They-will be very proud of you, as I am, dear old boy... Forgive me this once. Now say it." " Very well," Ee replied, moodily. "Only you must not. keep on bothering me, you know. Let it remain quiet for a bit, say, another six months." 6,6 -Douglas, how cals -I-keep silence that. long ? " "A difficult task, I know, for a woman. But I mast ask you to do it, and drop a line to your people telling them .not to mention a word. News of that kg in travels very quickly, and it would ruin everything not now if it leaked out that I was engaged to be married. All my plans would be ruined. But there, women Can't understand the reason of things. They don't know business has to be regarded as well love. But you can trust me ?" "With my life." Ho brought her up through the wood, and could hardly restrain his impatience, so anxious was he to be alone that he might break open the letter and avail hims•lf of its contents. He left her at the stile leading into the main road, for he was anxious that no tattling rustic should see him in her society. "Good-bye, Bitty," he said. "I inn afraid I shall not be able to eee you for a time. I shall be engaged with my lawyer each evening for some week or two."</p>
        <p>She pouted. This business seemed a very sudden affair. During the last week he had met her regularly night after night, and had poured into her foolish, willing ear a romantic love story. it had seemed such a delightful story, so like the novels or the fairy stories of her youth. He was her prince and she the Maid of low degree, and if it were not a ' kingdom she was to rule over by-and-bye, at least it would be a parish, for the word of the dwellers at the Chase had:Veen parochial law for manryeari and was well established and recognised. She, Kitty Lane, the poor telegraphist and village assistant-postmistress, daughter of a struggling linen draper in a small country town miles away, to be the squire's wife, the. grand lady at the Chase! Her giddy, innocent, pretty head was al ()got her turned at the prospect. "Is the lawyer .more important than L?" she asked, with affected petulancy. She had just risked penal servitude to gratifra whim of his. It seemed to her but an easy return to transact his legal matters during the day and devote his evenings, the only time she had to spare, to her: "I'm afraid it's impossible to go fully into all my business transactions at this moment," he replied, testily. "How is it you cannot believe me? Good-night." He turned abruptly on his heel and strode away. She called once after him, "Douglas!" but he affected not to bear. Tears welled up to her eyes. She was a gentle, weak, foolish, timid creature, and the man, with supreme selfishness, had not only stirred her pride in the delusive hope that piti would one day be mistress at the Chase, but had played upon her tenderestsensibilities and won her heart. An angry word from him was a dagger- thrust to her heart. She turned back on the road 'leading to the village and found her way to the little post-office where she lodged, a strange cloud resting upon her. She was a thief, a dishonourable thief. It had been done for him, that hateful act which, try SP she could now that she was alone, she Quaid rreo net out of her mind, and she I..new</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CHAPTER XV.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XV.</p>
        <p>SALVATION AND RUM</p>
        <p>"No letter, my child ? " Grace had just come in from the garden. Morning after morning had she watched for the postman at the garden gate, and always had she been disappointed. There were deep lines—lines scored by tearful anxiety—already beginning to mark the fair and even beauty of her face, aud the eyes were sunken as though by much secret crying. "No, father. Nothing for us." She spoke as cheerfully as she could, though her heart was nigh on breaking. But then she was young girl, and her father was an old man; and she was stronger, she felt, to the bearing of trouble than was he. Her kindly nature sought to lighten his load even though she might have to carry double.</p>
        <p>Day after day had passed without any sign of a silver lining to brighten their cloud. Old Hendon had utterly lost heart, and had become somewhat morbid, sitting all day by the side of the fire like, a man who has given up hope, and but . waits for the final cataclysm that shall fling him from the brink of the ruin beneath him right into the vortex of it. It would have been better for the last evil hour to have come at once than that he should have gone on day by day living in dread that every morning would see the blow fall. The farm was going to pieces, for the hands did pretty much as they liked. One or two faithful men went about their accustomed duty as usual ; but for the rest they were mere eye-servants, and had no other interest in the farm, its .success or otherwise, than that which appertained to pay-day. Grace and her father had not finished breakfast when the clanging of the gate and the barking of the dog indicated a visitor. Unconsciously the old man felt a trembling in his limbs. He lived in momentary dread of the appearance of bailiffs, and every unaccustomed footstep was a terror to him. "Why, it's Mr. Low," said Grace, who had leaped from her seat and taken observations from the window. "You had better see him in the parlour, father." The old man rose to receive his early guest with mingled feelings of doubt, but the jovial smile Low wore at once disarmed the fear he felt.</p>
        <p>"I've had such a stroke of good luck for yon, Hendon."</p>
        <p>It would have done an honest man good to have seen the quick light of happy, hopeful surprise that lit up the farmer's features. Low observed/ it, and inwardly gloated over the success he calculated would attend this extraordinary "stroke of luck" which he had carefully arranged for the old man.</p>
        <p>"A friend of mine," he said, "is rather flush of cash just now and wants to put a little of it out at easy interest." The farmer's face fell.</p>
        <p>"This is of little use to me, Mr. Low. I have told you before that I am mortgaged up to the hilt. I couldn't borrow a twenty-pound note." "Who spoke of mortgage ? " replied Low, cheer- fully. "Look here. 1 think two thousand pounds down just now at this crisis will keep your head above water. You have told me that much:" "That is so."</p>
        <p>Fou-r-a—nd:a-lialf interest would not hurt you." "I could manage to pay that if I only had the money." "Very well, then," said Low, with supreme magnanimity, "there's your cheque," and he dis- played the two thousand pounds cheque which be had that morning received from Lawyer Pryke. "But—"</p>
        <p>"There are no buts at all about it," said Low. "I hold myself personally responsible. The friend who is lending me the money does it as a personal favour. No, I will listen to no thanks. Pay the interest through use and as best snits you. No, I won't listen to it. Good-morning." Tie had played his part well—that of the generous benefactor, the saviour of Fenwick Barton and every living being it contained. The old farmer was in tears of gratitude. Hie heart. went out in great beats of overflowing thankfulness to this man who so opportunely had stepped in and rescued him and Gracie from the degradation which loomed before them.</p>
        <p>"Come, my child," he said, as Grace, hearing the door close and seeing Low hurrying down the garden, entered the little front room. "Come, my child. We are saved. The farm will not have to go. God has heard our cry of distress. Let us thank Him."</p>
        <p>Together they fell upon their knees, and the old man, with his arm about the best-loved of all his earthly possessions, poured out his heart in a prayer of thanksgiving such as had not crossed his lips for many a long day. After that it seemed as though brighter days were in store, though the effects of the fire very soon swamped up the two thousand pounds to satisfy hungry creditors. Farmer Hendon, with a strength and goodwill as of returning youth, set to work once more to retrieve his failing fortunes. Little dreamt he that the man who, had just come to his rescue was be who bad negotiated his ruin, and that the nun .had been schemed Solely that he might pose as a benefactor, and heap an unpayable obligation upon the fanner's shoulders. Nor did he reckon upon the unexpected difficulties of the future. And then suddenly, without any warning, Lawyer Pryke came down on him like a wolf upon the fold, with a ieotice to foreclose the greater portion of the mortgage money field upon the farm. The blow was a terrible one, for almost every penny of the two thousand pounds was gone, and even the whole of that sum would have been of little nee in clearing the heavy liability which had rested for some years upon the Barton. In vain did the farmer, plead. Lawyer Pryke's client, so vowed the lawyer, must have the money. The farm had been is very doubtful security for years past, and he. hid not pressed' for payment, hoping that better times would come and it could be made more convenient to Mr. Hendon. But money was urgently needed, and his client had no other course but to foreeloee. "But," added the lawyer, with the utmost die- interestedness, "there is just the possibility of raising another loan. The farm has depreciated it value, it is true, but I daresay I can find another mortittgee." Had it not been for Low's generous conduct in lending e the two thousand pounds, Farmer Hendon would have given up the struggle at this last straw. His bait had been bent to breaking-point. But. if he allowed the mortgagee to come in and Fell up everything how could he ever repay Low, who had trusted him without security of any kind He felt that he could never look either Douglas or his neighbours in the face again were he capable of such a piece of business. Low had known this, He had worked a fine coup when he bartered his cousin's pictures for the money that was to buy old Hendon, and, he hoped, his daughter. lie had them in his grip now and he meant to make good use of hiiropportunity. The farmer accepted Pryke's offer, and that wily lawyer simply transferred the property from himself, known as Jenkins, the first mortgagee, to himself, known as Roberts, the subsequent mortgagee, and pocketed a cheque of nearly a hundred pounds in payment of a complicated bill of fees and duties and stamps in the carrying out of the transaction. . h .</p>
        <p>The loss on this transfer was just sufficient to plunge the farmer below the tide level again, and the struggle became harder and harder. Thus things went on until Christmas. The pinch had become a personal one, affecting the household economy. Crace and her father, drawing the line at its tightest strain, had even known what it was to pinch for food. It was at this juncture that Douglas Low re- commenced to storm the outworks of Grace's heart.</p>
        <p>. • • • • • • • • • Christmas Eve. A storm of wind and snow had prevailed all day. The trees stood out upon the naked, cotmterpaned hillside like great starved ghosts ; the snow lay heaped around the farm build- ings in uncertain, uneven drifts. Inside the hous, a huge log burnt brightly, and in the flickerin: light of it sat Grace Hendon and Douglas Low. He had been on such friendly terms with the Hendons these last two mouths, and had grown so tvecustomed in his own mind to the belief that since Grace had nut heard from Martin and had not openly sorrowed over the fact she had forgotten him, that he had resolved once more to tempt fate and try his fortune. It was an easy matter to persuade the old farmer to sanction this course.</p>
        <p>"Spak to her," he had eaid, "if you are a* minded. I ,cill not say you nay. and shall be proud to weirome you ii Grace says ' yea.' But it must be of ber own free will. I am deeply indebied to you, Mr. Low, but no debt in the world would urre me to say one word to l'orr</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>unwilling consent from her. J.;ut say what yall will to her. Believing as he now did ir Low it was no wonder that the old man liarbot....ed a secret hops that Grace would by this time have forgotten her lover, a thing which in itself the fartaer would have welcomed, seeing that, in common with many others, he had believed the evidc e aninst Martin. As Low had expressed his intent on of asking the fatal question that night the old father discreetly, as he thought, took himself away. Grace knew by instinct what Low was about to say. Her breast heaved convulsively as he com- menced, for she knew that her task was harder than ever. This man held her and her father as in the hollow of his hand. How could she answer him? Low commenced with a reference to the times he had been rejected. "I thought," said he, "that if I waited my time there might, at some future date, be some hope for me. Have I waited long enough? Cannot I speak now what I have been longing to speak for so many weary months? Cannot I ape (.)Nv that you will at least grant me some small measure of love in return for that which I swear I _ have ever felt for you? " Grace's lips were twitching. Her breath seemed to catch in her throat. "0 Mr. Low," she said. "Don't yon under- stand? Have you forgotten n li..t I said when you asked me that first time ? Yu evidently do not know me. Isn't it enough that lam engaged to another ? The very fact that I could listen to your proposal, that I could accept you would make me utterly unworthy of you or any other man. It can never be. Whilst Martin lives—" "But does he live ? " broke in Low. "Have you heard from him? I had expected to get a letter from him three months ago but have heard nothing. He is either dead or in disgrace." • "I believe he is neither," said the girl. "Mr. Low, do not prolong this conversation, I beg you." "But Grace—" He rose from his seat to cross to her side and instinctive fear she rose also. As she did so she beheld a sight at the back of Low that seemed to freeze the very marrow in ber bones. The blind was not wholly drawn, and in the uncovered corner of the window appeared a face, a grizzled, sharp-lined face, with white, staring eyeballs, and a hunted, staiN ing look in every feature.</p>
        <p>in be caniinaed.)</p>
