        <p>WHAT ONE MAN SAYS :-</p>
        <p>I have been shipwrecked no less than three times. I had a severe attack of Rheumatism after the first wreck, and it became worse and worse. I suffered very much from stiffness and shooting pains in my legs, and for months I had to go about on crutches. I consulted the best medical men, but their medicines gave me only temporary relief. In vain I tried every sort of remedy that people could recommend. When reading the newspapers I had often noticed instances of people being cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, but had not paid much attention at first. One particular cure interested me very much, and lat last decided to try the pills. That was about four years ago.. I took the contents of two boxes, and felt a great deal better; I took another and another, until by the time I had taken six boxes I was completely cured. I tell all my friends what cured me, and shall willingly tell anybody who cares to ask me. I am glad you will be able to make known what Dr. Williams' Pink Pills did for me." As Mr. Thomas accompanied the reporter to the door he walked with a lightness of step and appeared so healthily buoyant that the visitor could scarcely believe he was talking to the father of a grown-up family; still less could he conceive the same man only able to move about with the assistance of crutches.—Mß JAMES THOMAS, a marine engineer, living at 5. Copper Street, Roath, Cardiff—to a reporter of the Western Mail.</p>
        <p>Thousands upon Thousands of men and women have testified to the curative Value of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills in the following ailments :—Rheumatism, Anemia," Decline," Consumption, Eczema, Scrofula, Sciatica, .St. Vitus' Dance, Indigestion, Biliousness, Palpitations, Paralysis, Locomotor Ataxy, Nervous Ailments, Disorders of the Blood The Pills are obtainable of every dealer, or from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company; Holborn Viaduct, London, price 25..9d. a box, or six boxes for 13s. gd.; but if you value your health, refuse every package that does not bear the full name (seven-words) in red on a pink wrapper, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Substitutes are sometimes grered by unscrupulous dealers: but they do not cure. But the true Pills will keep youist</p>
        <p>A RUNCOrtN OYSI ER SHOP.</p>
        <p>I OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Buckley-street P.S.A. Society. An open meeting of this Society was held in Buckley- street Free Mathodist Church on Sunday. Tee chair was occupied by Mr A Scott,senr., and the address was riven by Mrs Simpson. Solos were rendered by Miss Ethel Kane and Mc .1 H Cook, the accompanist being Mr A 'Crow. Holy Trinity Churches. The annual sermons on behalf ot the Sunday !reboot were preached on Sunday. In the morning the Vicar (Ray E C E Carleton), occupied the pulpit. The Roo. I' N Carter, M.A., preached at the afternoon services and in the evening the Rev W Pollock ttin,m A.. vicar of Palmate was the preacher. Suitable litmus were sung, and collections were taken in aid of the Sunday school funds. Cairo-street Sunday. School. There were large congregations at Cairo-street Chapel on Sunday, when the anniversary services in connection with the Sunday School were held, The Rev W H Drumtnond, of Balfaet, who until recently was pastor at the church was the preacher and his eloquent discourses were listened to with rapt attention. The music al por- tion of the services was most enjoyable. Special hymns were sung. and at the morning service the choir ably rendered the late Sir A. Sullivan's beautiful anthem "0 tote the Lord." In the evening they gave an expressive interpretation of the "Evening Prayer" (E E Truette.) A well-attended children's service was held in the after- noon, being conducted by Mr W Evans, of Liscard, a form-r superintendent of the school. Collections were made on behalf of the schocil funds, and • satisfact Dry ' , amount was realized. Brick-street Sunday, School Anniver- sary: This event was celebrated in the Brick-street Ind.- Pendent Methodist Chanel on Sunday, when sermons were preached by Mr Wibert fl'inshall. In the after- noon an address wag delivered by Mr Themes S. Steel to, a large convegatiou. After making eyeepsthetic refer- ence to the loss the church had sustained in the death a Mr Thomas Mayhall, who had been connected with the • schc ice and chapel for a number of years. Mr Steel drew some thatructive lessons to be learnt from a penny. , basing his remers upon the word., "Show me a ' penny," He urged the children to be truthful, to lead useful and upright lives, and to be obedien '. pure and holy. The address was listened to with attention and was much appreciated. The singing of the children was rs m.:rk,d feature of each eervice, and in fleeted great, credit upon the conductor, Mr William Barton. Return of the Rev. B. Percival. • The Rev, B. Percival, vicar of S. Petvea, has returned home from Sere z-rland, alter an at:dance of about nine months, and we are pleased to state be has Rua ly bench ted in health.. He received a hearty welcome from his parishioners and neighbours. Before commes clue his sermon on Subday morning, the Vicar said is had been a considerable trial to him and his family to be away for such • long time. ELI was sorry 'bat he had heir] unable to be amongst them to assist in the hard work which had been carried on in that large parish. Flo thanked God that He had seen fit to restore him to health and strength, and he trusted it would be Ms will to spare him to carry on his work in the parish. He wished to tharik all very heartily for the manner in which they had rallied round his c9lleai, uo, the Rev. W, Bracecemp. It was a great comfort to ' know that be had left someone in charge wbo would obrry on the work loyalty, faithfully and well, which Mr Bracecamp bad done. Working Men's Mission Sunday • School, Bank Quay. The annivrirsary services in connect Ann with the above Sundry sch eel were held on Sunday last. eaten the preacher in the morning and evening was Mr S G Rogers, of Didebury College, Manchester. In the after- noon a Service of Song, entitled "In the time of Daises" was rendered by the children and choir, the conr.ectiye readings, bekg given in an effective mincer by Mr H Wallington. The singing ttroukhout the day was very creditable, and the choirmaster (Mr S Heiman) is deserving of praise for the manner in which he bad trained the children. Solos wire rendered by Mis w Taylor, Miss H Oakley, Miss F. Hassell. and Mr Millington, Mr F Atkinson presided at the organ. The ongri gations at each sorvme were very satisfactory, 1 cnd the collections for the di y amounted to 10 3a Bd. --...-- FLD FATALITY AT UELSIIY.--at the Chester Infirmary. on Tuesday, Mr E Massey (coroner) held an inquest on the body of Fredsrick Edward Sutton, aged three.— Deceased was playing on the road near Elelsbv School when be was knocked down by • shandry, driven by Edwin J( ft+, welch paseel over the Child's body, itifiTt- leg serious internal injuries, from which be succumb d Immediately on admission to the infirmary on Monday 1 vening.—The. jury teturned a verdict of "Accidental death</p>
        <p>What the Police Raid Disclosed.</p>
        <p>Messy! John Ockleston at d Edwin Imison, sitting at the Runcorn Sessions, on Mentlay, wi re natio' upoa to aejudicate in a case in which Edward Bioder, an oyster dealer of 50, Regent-street, Runcorn, was charged on re- mand with using his noose fir the purphses of betting with persoos resouing there, on the 5h of May and divers other dates. Mr J.ist ph Burton, solicitor, Run- corn, acted for the police. Prisoner who elected to be tried by the Court,pleaded guilty. Pulize-Sergeant William Brewster, of Runcorn, swnre, that in con-eq ience of in tilt :thins received be attended the defendant'a !louse, 50, R igent-street, on Saturday afternoon last, at ten Inioutes past thri e. Be saw the defendant there and Acziog-Sergeant Gregory, and Was shortly afterwards j intd by the Superiutendent. Gregory handed him a search warrant which be read over to the defendant. The warrant had been taken out in cons e.; ience of information tec-ived. because it was thought the house was used for b•tting puposes. On searching the defendant he found 52 slips of piper, 3 s, and 2 brinks relatirg to betting (produced). Also the aunt ot £lO 7s 111 chiefly in silver and copper. The rest consisted of 30i in gold and a two shilling pos. tal order. Sergeant Gregory found a basket under the table containing 60 packets of money wrapped in paper showing the names and the act ouots, and the names of the horses backed. The whole of the 52 slips found on the defendant's person related to races run that day —Rempton Park and Ripon—witk the ple ption of two runs at Chester on the 3ch, and one for the "City and Suburban" run last month. The whole of the 60 slips in the basket related to races) run that day. The t.itai number of betting slips was 110 and represented £2O 10, di. Defendant was the tenant of the house and shop 50. Regent-street, which was supposed to be an oyster shop, but the onll o 3 s• ere he had seen in the place were jest a few in the window. Tie Superintendent Raid to the defendant's niece, who was in the shop, "You had better get reauy to vim to the Rilice Saation." The defendant then sad Por God's sake don't lock her up, for I am rc- sponsib!e for everything done in the hou-e, The money , and slips she tattoo, the does on my instruction. She knows nothing about betting." The defendant was then sent to the Police Station in charge Of Police- ' Constables Beeler and Et wards. Wit aess afterwards saw him and read the sworn information to him. Us replied "It is all right I have takes bets, hut! am very glad you have not atreeil my niece." The same aftei - noon he was brought up before the magistrate and when asked by the clerk to snow any raison why he should not he remanded, he replied " Ni, I am guilty." Witness had knnwn the defendant for the last six years, and had known him to be a book-maker all the time.</p>
        <p>Acting .Sergeant Gregory swore to keeping watch from 1.55 to 3 5 p.m., on the S ur doting which time he saw 31 persons go no the shop. He went into the shop shortly before 3 10 p.m., and was subg(guently joined by the Sus eririrendma, Berg.: ant Brewster. and Constables Bowyer and Elwerd4. In the luck bed- room, between the bed and thl mattress he found sev, n paper bundles which contained 270 betting slips. (oro- dUtterl), tint they were not of reeent date. He cotrub raed the previous witness, and stated that he had worked out the betting ac..vording to the results in the papers, and the defendant would havo to par eut. a matter of gz IJ. leav!ng a bal*ne4 of £l2 144 61. The betting ' was carried on in stly with thti, working classes. Deferidknt :Yon have not taken the am idler betts jute ene calculation. The place betting?,, Witness, No. 'D fenclant : Dien that will make a dlfhirenee. I will have to pay out more. .Defendatit pleaded that be had been very unlucky,and bad. 1.04 a cons,derable rum as his banking account would show. He tind ben 25 yearn in the town, and it we the .first time be bad ever been before the court in his life,' ' DIV Bench. after a short retirement, fined the defend- ant elo and 8. 61 Costa, and advocates fee £1 Is, or in default two montbs with hard labour. The money was paid. ' •</p>
