      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MR. JAMES STEELE, OF RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MR. JAMES STEELE, OF RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>This week we record the death, in his 46:h year of Mr James Steele, of Runcorn, which took place at 99, Highgate, Kendal, on Saturday last. Decriaaed had been ailing , for Over twelve months, and had sought to Irecruit himself by a change of air. F r ROM time he resided at Alvanley, liel-by, and lately he had been living at K-ndal. These changes, however, did not effect any p-rmanent improvement in his health, and his condition gradually became worse, death taking place. as stated, on Saturday last. Deceased was a victim of phthisis. Toe lato Mr Steele was w, II known in Run- corn by reason of his business connection with the town as a draper and miliiner, during a period Of tiOnle 16 or 17 years. For the greater par of that period, his prin- cipal establish= n': was in Courch-street, but some years ago, he opened a branch grip in Oreenwarroad. Owing to failing health, ho gave up the Church-street business, at Cnristmas last, and took into partnership at his Greenway-road establtshment, Mr Robinson, a former manager. Mr Steele took no active part in public affairs, but, inc mnection with St. John's Presby- terian Cnurch, be was a most active and energetis worker For many years he sled the position of deacon, and every movement in connection with the church, had his most cordial support, lie was one of the foremost in the efforts which have been made, from time to time, to rake Wads, for the building of a church, in V ctoris-road, and before his death he bad the satisfaction of knowing that the greater portion of the money had Nren raised towards this object. Mr Steele was a native of Annan, in Scot- land, and was unmarried —At the St Jahn's Presby- terian Onurch, Runcorn, on Sunday morning the preacher, the Rev T Sydney Morris referred to the lois the church had sustained in the death of Mr James Strele. The decease i gentleman had always taken a great interest in the church work. He had looked for wirzri h•mefully to melee the new church erected. Be would be sadly missed by them, having been with them for about 21 years. The funeral took place at Annan, Scotland, on Wednesday.</p>
