        <head>QUICK CHANGE ARTIST.</head>
        <p>THE QUICK CHANGE ARTIST.</p>
        <p>lie's a smart man. I know one who played the put of six characters in s'xteen minutes, in a little play of his own. In that time he acted the puts of a, miser. a German tnesician, a lover, a dowager, a footman and the heroine.</p>
        <p>Mrs J Stevens, of Wool House, Ai, Nr. Wronalln, Kent, when in ber prime, in reality lived the part of feeble old wo:nan ; hobbling about on crutches, quitsi unable to kneel or stonp, frightened to es', dropsical. entirrly miserable and full of pains and diseases—that was picture number One. Two aside for six bti•f weeks. Hey, presto! Mrs, Stevens is in her prime now. Son is happy, takes a walk early in the morning, and a thrrmile jaunt in the evening, sleeps like a top and eats heartily.</p>
        <p>That quick change was made' by Mother Ssigel's Syrup—lrom bad dreams arid heavy night sweats tn calm and refreshing lumber.</p>
        <p>On August 23,d, 1900, Mrs. Stevens wrote .—" I was sultct to sick headaches from q ii'e a girl. N ) matter what I ate, I was tormented witn pains across my chest, in the small of my back, and a fearful dull pain in my side, as though socne,ne was trying to pull me down. My heart used to flu' ter like a tiinorous bird, and my brea,hing wee that bad I could not walk upstairs was weary and worn out, :tui cot worse aud worse. " Dropsy set in and my lirnbi grew twice their natural size. The d'ictir and the U ;RAW folks both pronounced me as incurable, and for three years / baa to bobble about en sticks.</p>
        <p>"At hie the d'etcr advi4ed a real good trial or, Seigera Curative Symp. I got N bottle.</p>
        <p>" When that wae done I got another and another. At the third bottle the dropsy showed signs of leaving me. To make a long story short, the p tine gradually left tne,and at the end of six months I threw aside my. sticks and did my hommworir with ease and comfort.' A gectltmln living in Aeh (Mr Lynch) says " These facts are ones for which I c tn vouch."</p>
        <p>The quie est of quick change artists is N dare. Otule yen allow poisons to mix with your blood, which begin to show their presence by bead ich-s. wind-spaams, cleanliness. neuralgia, yellow skin, &amp; Lc, and Nature will in a Ash change you from a be4ltby sub' 3et to decrepit, disease-ridden min or Woniiin.</p>
