      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>THE EX A MINER. Iltit Mb, 1901.</p>
        <p>Fr will be seen the more ills Education Bill. closely the Government Bin- cation Bill is scrutinised that Whatever advantages it has to recommend it are Sore than conntionalanced by defects and dis- advantages which against the object Which the measure has professedly in view. If we tike even the clause which enables School Boards eontinue the work of higher education a hich thoY have bugue, it will be s -en that they can ?ttlY do this as agents of the new authority and on Lta terms apd conditions. To place a popu- -44Y ciliated body, charged with such impor- tant duties under the control of a selected cOnamittee of county gentlem-n who will act ea a kind of Scum) of Lords, dies W. Cit seeril to promise much in the way of iurther- Ing the interests of that body. Tie Bill professes ‘°oontinue the Slhool Beards prautic illy in the tall+ Position as before, but this is a more blind. 'go whole urrangtiment of tie scheme of the 00Yernment mailifestly contemplates the ultimate eatinction of the School B iards, and this, we rght be siire,will c-one at the eerliest opportunity. Ilia therefore necessary to view the hearing of the ill as a whole, because once committed to its proi- eiPleit will be difficult, if not impossible, for the tiation to gut rid of it when&amp; complete system of Onelation s plac.id before the country. This at c,rite tlis ciliation of whether edacatitm 14 a subject which can he safely delegated to a couaty co ninittee, or does its importance tall for the cqsa.,ion of an ad hoe bociy ? It Wait bq said that tho ad hoc body has received scant cOurtesy r the hen is f the G ivoinne it and its 41" d% Sr JoaN GJR,T referred to it as a dia- etedited ins itutooo, but he advanied no evidence the statement. Trio tine in which he referred :the work of the School B damning them ' ""th faint pra:se, and scoffingeven at the c.iililren's Ztertain mint, which they had provided at own expense, isties not indicate A ttittitl Of IdOti in which* a just conclusion 4;40 be arriv.:td at On ths subjsct. It IV be true that we cannot erect the School i7uard into an authority for all ki ills of education' h:t the fp istion is whether the birly which is to p erected p-operly ereiody the principles of • repr is intatio s and orlanic growth which itc. the vital elements respect;valy in the S ;hoof thiatrci system and the work it has carried oil. Are b !lines :in which the fownditions of the new aro to h) laid down to be chosen from exp irienco d mIC d M the department of eln- 04it.ion itself,or in that of the multifarious interests 1 government generally ? Toe latter el- to a period of ten years inore or less, tha tloler rua;la, ir beyond the Liiir:y years during Ic4 a national system of edivi.ition hall existed in this country. Right ,Qticural,l3 gentlemen in discussing this sub- o 'lcat f • 4 rg it th it education is very ranch m oil than tio-48ro local qaestiou. It is a great national ques- trt:i Americ Ga:inaoy, our greatest r,vals in 44.;* hro also oar greatest rivals in education ; w we are as slow in learnieg from their methods bit tho one as in the other. Ta.y have their lt4.reaus of adue„tio; we have Our COMOOtteO Of ejllooil, or Educatiou Department. Is it 4C°Ol liable to assert that the work of this great urtnent of the State should filter through con. 4,o4'ne local holies, elected for purposts of surd- - tievt‘l' 41,,neajw.r., .a rotes? Seals is 144 INotokens a poo conception of the treadsuf lilik"f• It nay be all nu well $0 bring oraor</p>
        <p>' out of chaos, but in doing so care most ho taken not to leave behind all that was worth the having. • Otherwise the new order may be symmetrical enouoh,but at the same thou it may b the shadow I r, • without the substance.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of The Bradford Conference. the National Liberal Federation at Bradford has had a geod send off. The meeting is one of the largest that has,ever been hold ; the welcome has I been cordial,and the opening proceedings interest- ing and full id promise. Dr STENCH WATSON in his presidential address referred to the difficulties that still lie in the path of the Liberal Party in bravo and becoming terms. There cen be no doubt that the party iTstill in the broken seas that formed the wake of the great split )f 1886 ; but it is a fair indication that the worst deys of trial are over I when men in the position of the Federation's president take such an excellent grasp of the situa- tion. Difficulties, as he says, are the salt of life,l and without thorn no strong or wholesome charac- ter can be evolved. The party having, by the force of circumstances, become attached to one great leader, personally and intellectually as well ,as politically, has had the experience which was ! bound to follow when it was compelled to slip its moorings. It has been tossed about on the wave of eouftcting opiniens,as if it possessed neither pilot nor, compass, nor rudder. But this lamenteble I condition was more apparent than real,a.s troubles I are apt to be exaggerated while one is in the throes of their agony. Now that the sails are being I trimmed, the damage repaired, and a favourable; breeze has sprung up, there is a rising hope of a prosperous voyage and a safe anch.irage at no very distant date. Dr SPENCe WATSON struck the triml key note when he said that "the great mass of the Liberal party were determined to sta id closely together, in support of true Liberal principles." It is in devotion to principle that victory is to be secured; and if it be true as the president hopes and believes, that in the days of their adversity I Liberals have learned to bear arid forbear, and to banish personal qaestions, there should not be any insuperable difficulty in present- ing the united front to the enemy which will ensure success. Liberal principles are as patent as ever, and quite as attractive to the ; thinking politician. The mists of selfishness will occasionally obscure the vision. Bat a few more object lessons such as the present government ars ! giving, in the direful results of a policy dictated by pure undieguised selfishness and greed will cleer away the fog, and lead to the presentati in in a stronger light than ever the higher excellence of those political doctrines which are founded upon the sure foundetions of integrity, j ostice land right.' Dr. Sri +WE WAT,ON referred in his address chiefly i to the general election, the method of carrying on the South • African War, the neglect by; the government of domestic matters, arid the quistion of national education. Tire last• named subject is favoured with several emergency resolutions on the agenda; and it is probable that round it will centre the chief interest of the meet- I ing. It presents such an admirable focus for the energies of the party, that it cannot be thought the meeting will miss such an opportunity of uniting its energies irs a determined straggle for the right. Not only is Scotlaed in advance of England in this matter, but according to the worthy president our trade rivals, the United Slates and Germany are so far ahead of us that though we may overtake them we cannot hope to beat them. We are beat even even by " Little Switzerland," which spends more money every year on the education of its people than it does over its army. That is a lesson Englishman can under.: stand ; will they remember it,and take it to heart?</p>
        <p>Again the feature of the inouthly tabour Reports of the B )ard of Trade is the overwhelm- ing preptider ince of strikes terminating in favour of the employer, as against those in which the workpeople succeed. It also appears that in the struggles on a small scale the workman is, on the average, far more successful than in thoie in which thousands of persons are implicated. Oa the whole, however, the warning given by the statis- tics for April is emphatic enough againat work- people embarking on ctrikes with a falling market, lif they can be in any way avoided. For trio labour 'market is still on the down grade, and though the decline in very few occupatioos ts really mark ld ' as compared with the state of things in Marco, it is stillsorious °lough, as foPowinga geoerallend- ency,and showing a falling-off as compared with the figures for a year ago, which is not at all pleasant to contemplate. With regard to rate of wagest 'few advances are reporteu, hut the de- creases are in an overwhelming majority, the weekly wages of 54,874 workpeoplis hasongdocliiied sn average 24 8,1. per head. Li Harch advaucas and aecreases almost ex may balanced each other; but to shew that the present state of things com- pares unfavourably with that existing a year ago it is enough to say that in April, 1900, the net result of changes was an &amp;ay:oleo of 11 51 I. in the average weekly wages of 273,400 workpeople. There is a decline in almost every empl lowa specified, but, happily, in no case is it really of a sensational character.</p>
        <p>That jaurnarstic mouument of learning and sobriety, the Times, is opening its columns to humour. It published the other day a letter from a young Frelichinan in a provincial tou of Franc I offering himself as a prize in a lmtery— )r, one might say, ir oposing *lottery with hiiaself es the prize. Ttlero wa.4 system j,ihjs tnethnd. Ha pro. posed to issue t ckets to the number of 25,000, tne price of each ticket to be £l.. Oily elgiuli ladies would bily,hacense otherwise they won d i.e throw- ing their money away without even a chance of retort,. 'rue young man undertook to dap- isit with 90,00 iesponsible person,implicitly to be relied upon to keep the secret, the nuinher which should 'be deemed the winning one. He reserved to him- Bit the right to refuse the lady; bat then he ' would also f.irfeit, save for a smut reward to he pall to him for having thought of the ida, the 25,000. As he stands at present lie is penniless, and his good figure, engagin:: manners, aryl exc -t- -ient he Ilth would not count in the desire for housekeepiov, yet, as he'says "J'ai resok de ma , manor." Hence the selietne to make himself dew- able. It reminds one of the story of the way in j which an architudes clerk in) a till firm obtained A share in the business and the hand 01st rich lady, by a twofold stroke. fie went to Vie lady's fathix, and was told that his station flrbadis the thought. "But Mr B.i-end-Si) is about to sdmit as P krt. ner." "Ali," said the father, "that Make.; a difference." Then he went to the he,d of the firm and asked for a partnership. "Preposterous !" was the answer; " you'd lower the ri-iput,istion of the basin-. 58." " But," he replied," I ain about to :become son-in-law of Soiatid-So." " 01, that . makes all the diff..rence;' said the head of tile firm ; and so tint poor young architect came to dwell in , high places.</p>
        <p>Manchester, Salford, and Strotforci, together with the L )cal G,..wern nmt Board, seam deter- i mined to exact a p rtiou of thn coat of th,t Port ' Sanitary Authority from Warrington and other urban ad rural authorities. At present the ad-, ; ratiragus to Warrington from bong a port mn the Stlip Canal are more imaginary than ohvions. Few veozole load (wr fiat-4d here, and the proposal ittiorvrew to make dm ba.ais of wadi:m.6on that of rideable value is distinctly unfair. :Vt.)? should Warrington pay one-thirteenth of the whole</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <p>i:</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRInAY, MAY 17, 1901</p>
        <p>expenses of the Port Sanitary Authority,whee the and (locirom." Toe Ki ‘g Divisien !.ace .1e town do's not snare in the wat .r-borne traffia ? dined o reverse chi jud:4men,,on t e griuni that tue haro-plsyin and toe dancing w a b..e.tch of Other authorities outside Mauchester have the 'good oruer and decorum," and, in nee ver, t .at, the same complaint to make, and the counter proposal dancing dust,which w Ls not unlikely to dam- that the tonnage of vessels calling at the various age tee. m.,ch Ary in. the room Toe moral, of ports should be the basis of contribution is un- course, th • There 1.. a plies for everything,and questionably mueh the fairer sini more equitab'e everything should be in its pl4ce." one. The Local G .vernment Board has, we be.. i lieve, consented to grant the order on the basis of rateable value, 8) that the aggrieved with°. rites will have to combine and contest the matter in Parliament if they are determined to resist the imposition. The Council was unanimous in supporting the views thus laid before it by Alderman Sutton and the Mayor, so that Warring- ton will be in the forefront of those who intend to resist the Local G.Nern went Baid's order. This tatifioning of backs all round appears perfectly justifiable, thratgh,as the total esti- mated expenses of the Port Sanitary Authority are only £632 for the 31, ar, it may be questioned whether all this bother abccit the payment of one-thirteenth is ra'ly politic and consistent with the principles of true economy.</p>
        <p>It is an encouraging " si,m of the times "that in many large provincial towns the housit g problem is just not' receiving marked att ent ion. It is examine at public meeting, forms a theme of innum,rable " letters t ) the papers," and of leading or, ides in bob n.wapapers and mag• rnes, and i approached in a practical spirit by hundreds of local committees scattered over the country. But in the one essent,al assembly whore it is the mo.t desirable that it should figure conapieunisly, that is to say in the flou.e of Commons, the sunj-ct finds no place. Yet Patliatnent cannot long ruin a deaf ear tithe 'voice of the country, in tlis war tsr,which is beginning to assert in no uncertain tones or doubtful phraseology.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Lord S lisbury is "teetotally" impracticable; "impossible" as some would ca ill t, on t .e temper- ance question As Lord Rosebery observed in the Upper H..use this week. a lit n the Bishop of Win- chester moved a 'resolution the noble Marquis said "Bring in a bill," and when a bill is brought in he says he will not look at it. It is very well, and amusing in its way, for the Premier to allow him- self the luxury of shocking the Bishops .ind sneer- ing at philanthropists. and to see the Bishops and some of his o A n followers who are really in earnest on the lic tv.ing question wringing their hands because of his wickedness, and their inability to make any impressiun on him. Buthere a point at which humour of this kind may become e - hausted, and that point, if report may be relied u on, has been well nigh reached 1.).d Camper- down had introduced on Tuesday evening a very modest measure indeed of licen ing reform; but Lord !•zalisbury would have none of it. He appeals to believe th t there should eno licensing laws at all. He probably knows nothi g about public- houses,and has not the faintest. ides of the ex es ive number of " flaunting gin palaces," as L rd Peel calls them, that are striking features of ell our larger towns, or of the conditions under which they are carried on Lord Hosebery said the Premier's speech made him despair. The s cond reading was eventually lost.</p>
        <p>' rxcitement is appar ntly again setting in, as it did two years since,over the possibility of bringing back to England the American Cup, an excitement in which the provinces will undoubtedly have a large share. But the probabili ies may, perhaps be forced into a new channel by the re tilt of the pre- sent week's race. The course was a thirty mile ne and the race is assumed to have been sailed under tolerably fair condition, as regarls weather! Aeco ding to the reports, the speeds were high hroughout the whco cant at, which ended in a victory for hamrock I, the vessel that failed to defeat the Defenderin Amer can Waters The finish was close, the older boat getting home with only five minutes and a few seconds to the good, ham- rock II being thus defeated by he sister yacht It would possibly be unfair to draw any important deduc ion- from this test race. A thormgh over- hauling of •ha Brock 11, may have the eff !ct of enhancing her merits; and she may, indeed, not have been put to her full speeds rhere a: e many possiblities to be conside ed before it is permiss- ible to give a definite opinion. !- ir Thomas Lipton hi self has a profound belief in the a ility of Shamrock II to bring away the trophy from the other side of the Atlantic, and wha is more, the exper a hold to the same opinion, and thi 4 confidence of the owner and of the men is not to be ugh ly set aside.</p>
        <p>Taxation imposed on art des of universal con- simption ma.; be good io its way, but it has sn .ftct upon thiune'al realuat merits in the m ,e• , unexptierei ai I p unthotight of places. The iinp,ist on sugar, to? in Lance, will toue,i t e con- densed milk of the babe al well as whit some political a ri•ato,2 ere p'ev4,d to call the " poor mati's heer." It will aff4et the luneheon hi•cuit of the adult, and t'us went f-iod of the in'ar, ; bat few p -ople would have thought that, oeyowl mak- ing the cup- of tea or cocoa a frac.ion dearer, it would i,-fl lone° to a considerable extent other beverages of he abstainer. Yet it is declared thie, the who'esale mineral water manufasrurere of biv,irpool and the di ,t.-ict have Ore tily in .reavid The price of their gold, by threep-bnce per it 2 in to meet the enhatwael Cl&lt; -ußar. El mct! tie re- tailers have reisnon!.ed by charging to the con-miner a half-n,nny a ly.tt.le extrs, and the latter has, as in these eases, to much more than psy the 1 wh it of he increase in t‘ii.a ,iirection.a4 well as the impost on other articles. Bat the eff et in yet ano•ber direction is not without its e!eni.int, of humour. F.lvi wo•il I have gu s.ied tha, the sugar tax would preju liCiallv attiet the boo -blackine in- dustry and the cot of the luxury of ft "p lieti " generally. But the pubic are now reminded that the main ingrediors of hot polish are molas,es end powdered chareoa! in a certain condition. The II tax on molasr-es, thonzh smallinvolv,slArg4 Ma'lll- I facturers of black,ng sod harnea4 clraposition in coq-idorable additi ,nsl expen-e, and it is said that ninny extra thousunds of poun is will in this vor,y be spread over our boots and -noes. But it requires another Samuel Welirto do justice to the suojec t —and t) the bootblack.</p>
        <p>Viey have ciriona ideas of cruelty in sern, parts of ip Prinipali y. A lit 'e while ago a farmer, named 1../w.411 J.ines, was 801)1 nons I for cruelty to a dog it Was Ststld 'lilt the animal was force" ti w rk at s sort c) treadmill, which was mid for elurning. The appara-us was so arranged that the dog could not stlei withlut being choltill It was urged for the defence that the work W,14 not heyon 1 the aniroare awl that it" was a w-11-known custom "in Wales tow irk these chiit by naansi of dogs The case was adjourned in or let that the magistrates might inswet the siparatti9 at w .rt. and now these g.sntlernen have neld that the defendant WO ) intetviol roioflfct ieltv, and " that. thowth churning was not to the digs in itinc,." it did not clo.ntiTue eru,t.v. [hie being t'ie rase. tht,ugh the tre elmil is "nt t a Iman's instinct," he vill in future have the coasola- tary r.ffection when climbing upstairs without getting near, r to he top that he is not doing too hard labour. at his c pantry's exp-ns-, and that there is no int:qt.-ion to inconvenience nin) to the verge of cruelty. Yet the difference will r• m lin that the dog labours only for Pile mater', profi ,at 1 the itniniu-nt risk of tieing ch Arai. wl&amp;e the man does ton emu,* by way of punistrne it., ml at no i risk, except to his shins, for positive crime.</p>
        <p>That therl was ne-,d of a 11u-banls' Proter!tir,o Society max' b-1 readily gran' e ; thAr4 is al,o wed of a wive,' Prlrecti,m Associ,,tion, and now tUe at its ..ec ml o,ifer-ncl tte former ble resolved to widen the 11114 ef its operations no as to include t yranti a' Niv,‘,4 as well SI th 44 drunkel, an twits- tion may be Pt uectol to stiiin4 op fmrttiqitu, titiAirt by cert ii ot the h ntrrm-roinden who ;or once will have some ckim neon pubic symnaihv. It may furl'her be auticipate I that ins ell of the fl I I of twion being limited to tVe.t- rnin•ter—luti Is Sr. S'e,ilien'n, be it add.d—the toov•ment will extend in bo'%h Cqfl'A to the Drfl- viune. eqn understand the Bishop of Wiuohes. ter. the Rome S.cretary, and the Lora (lief Jll4lCil aPknowlerlginz the Hoehn, d+' Pre'ectioe resolutions, but nr Baf nit—What nperial know• ledge can he be otipp ,sed t) have on the ,•ithle,t The Lord Chi fJustice, bv-tfie-hte, considers Vera w.)nlu be " very great diffirmlty in giving eff,cw to the r.,olotios," and po.,ebly the province, after the r'XtorensUa of r,pioion from such a quarter, will favour a waiting plicy fr.r the prasent.</p>
        <p>Not much is heard now-I- lays of proceedings under the Truck Act, which once were so common in the provinces. But an addition was mAJ, in the Ring'e Beech D.viaion the other &amp;iv, to the rulings under rho exirtinv ststrow A Bris,ol was proceeded esirpor4 by a 0 v-rons-nt factor . le- • p-ctor. because several glrlr had been fined fTr playing the harp and dancing in the factory during the dinner hour. The Gysernment inspector held that the imposilon ,pf these fines eorustitWed a contravention of the Truck Act of Ito 3. The case is pr ,bably unique in the litigious tnaalsof the factories and work-hops Bristol magittrates had held that the emplop•re were not rout rgreg the Art by iropasieg a fine, b wawa theta Wes a rale in force that while on the premiiee all employere shoall observe " good order</p>
