        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF • FORMILK RESIDENT. —Uzi Thursday , afternoau rest week the remain" or the late Mr Joseph ' Dairies, of Yr!atone UM, near farporiey, were interred at It. Frodsham Parish Carrell, in tirt presence of his son, Mr thomas Davies, a-'d a mimb-r of sorrowing relatives and friends. The Year, the Rev H B Blogg, olhoated. Years ago the dec ased was a member of the firm or Messrs (*.shell and Davies, of trio Frodsham Bridge fl tur mills. Sub equently he bad resided at Til- ston Mill House, where he and Ms son nave carried on a similar buttinesv. Alter a brief attack of bronchitis he died on 'T uesday morning last week agt d 78 years, grealy respected by all wno knew him. . . _</p>
        <p>Nzerarcri-No ars lcuthriars.—at the Frodeham Police Court, ou Monday, betore Ilifr Charles Reynolds and Mr John Murray, Alfred Thornley, of the Queen's Head- yard, Frodshata, was cnarged with having been drunk and disorderly on Si, urcay aft-rnoon last, and, on the evidence of Sergeant Hunt was fined 103.—The defend- 'ant WAR soosequeutly ctavged nv lispector Hackett, of the N.S.P.O C., with having on the 11 h May and at other times during the past s x months willully neglected to maintain his three children, 14 years, tine years, and three years of age ar Frodsham, whereby they had suffered in heslth.—Me Bra wne, who prosecu-ed on behalf of the society, said this was a case of wilful neg- -1 ct on the part of the defendant—persistent neglect to hand over to his wife his wages iN full or to:any extent that would be sufficient to k-ep the children in proper corichion, During the past five weeks be bad only banded her a total of 18, 6 and but for the kinchn as of the neighbours and the mother of his wife th child- ren would have been c rnpelled to go to the Workhouse. _flsviife, who is blind, preyed the neglect, and said that filthy language wa4 used by the defendant when drunk to heisell and the children. Her evidence was corroborated by neighbours who had fed the children daily for the last three months but were now tillable to do anything further.—lnspector Hackett deposed to finding the house in a perfectly clean condition.—The magistrates, aft r hearing what th police had to say, told the defendant that the neglect wss owing to drink, and that he wiuld be sent to gaol for a month with hard liboor.</p>
        <p>FRODSHAM METAL MERCHANT'S AFFAIRS.—At the Luidou &amp;krupcv Cu ts M inday, the a( jaurned ti-st meeting nt the creuitors took place before Mr Chipman. fficial r c-iver, under the failure of J L Mortice, 14. Nova Scitia, Mann Hand,Liverpol, resid- ing at Bradley, f4o.istram, m“tal agent and merchant, m ire rereatly of 34 and 36, Gresham-soreet. Lendon, nommisaion scent and agent tor the Hagfish Eleotro Me'aflurgical Company of Leeds, L,mited. It appears that the debtor 1415 ft, d in businet.l at Liverpool SOW four or five years ago, without capi. b drag financed by aMr II Brd Wile in, n 1 Liverro.d, upon terms of 12 re'r cnt and ash •re f t,e profi s. Same time after wards Mr Wilson sold his businees to a limited company with the rPqult that he loat hql financial aPsistance and lie atruitizt don till M 1899,.n 1 n gold it to Messrs J G Brown and Cyf L vertu) )1 fo• £3OO He afterwards went tn London and started a • a commission agent, and cootinueil it up to the Lim. that the r. c: icing order was made upon the petition of P.n.'s B ink, Limited, credit- ors for £2OO. tie attribiar.d his ins ,Ivency to the failure of financial support fr,rn Mr Bird Wilson, to pressure he creditors, and t failure to nbtain a petition in the Elecrro Met,llurginal Company, L4mit.d, whin', he had been promised. Pr flis to the amount of £3,075 were dealt with, and reiolutioqe were I:I:wed leav,n4 the ease ing the hands of the official receiver to wind up in baLkruptcy.</p>
