        <head>-•-. DEATH OF A WARRINGTONIAN IN AMERICA. ,</head>
        <p>-•-. DEATH OF A WARRINGTONIAN IN AMERICA. ,</p>
        <p>We regret to hear of the death of Mr Peter !Shaw second sin of Mrs &amp;law. Be wsey-street,Warrington, whin') tr,curred su Mealy at Brookl3n. New York, on t:e first of the present m Infh. Ths sad event was eniirely uncap cterl by his relatives, as until quite recently be had be.n in robust teJlth. The CaUS3 of deir.h was peritonitis. The ()noosed who was forty-tine years of ege.was born in Claiborne- street, Warrington. Piior t.. leavng England ter America, sixteen years ago he was well known in Warrington, and w s h ghly respected by a large circle of iriend.. In the au nein of lit•t }ear lie came over to Bogiaerl fir a holiday, and visited W..rrington. Two hr there and a si.ter rurvive him. On. • f his br., hers i • ex-"-lergesnt Shaw, of the Warrington Police Force, and the other Sergeaot-Maj ir h. wof the Highland Light In- fentrvot p--satit statione as Aldershot. Mrs lids- wicic.of fiew-ey-triet. is his only surviving sister. T.,61 following obituary notice appeired in the Greenpoint WeeklyS ar" of Sa•urd,y May 4tl : " After an ille&amp;s of • nly a few dais Peer Shew died on Weduesday at his late residence. 650, Leottird•stteet. Pent mils was the cause of death. Mr Saw was of athletic f^rm ant built and was a flee ep4cimen of manhoorl. He was born at Man- ehertsr (Warringtoo) Engles) jut over forty nine years ago and oa) resided n Greoneoint about 16 yew. Just previous to c •ming here he resided In Canada about one year. Ile was thor- oughly Engli,la and was an outspoken en- thusiast concerting old Enelaiid ; its traditions its people, etc. Ha never became • side -n of the United States, as be hoped soWe (ley to return to Enlziand to live. He ;rent home last year and spont a delightful time among relatives and friends. Mr Shaw was the biritoiie soloiet of the vested choir at the Garden City CatheArtil and possessed voice of rare ‘ole•ne and .highly callvired. Ha was a.i export mould maker in he employ of E P 011ason and C 1., Cornmerc a!-street, and was held in high ri-'e in generally. There are few men in Brooklyn of Mr Shaw's age who showed such evidences of vigour or gave promise of so many years of life. But he was cut; &amp;me in the trime of life with scarcely any premonition whatever, having bet il ill just one week. Fie had been a M9rnaer of t e NaseniC fraternity about one veer having been raised in Baltic Lo:'ge No 281. He also became a Royal Arch</p>
        <p>The sudden d-ath of Mr Shaw has caused a severe shock to his many friends, an I the wife and daughter who survive him have the qi•mpa hy aed cond Oence of tn. ir friends and the genet .1 public as well,in the terlible lose they I are enstsinei. The funeral Nerviee was held at rt e family reridence, 650. leeonarii-ereet, last nig .t. It was largely ottendsd the m-mbers of 13.1ric 10,-/go, No 278, and tie Arch Masons, and the Brooklyn Cimera Club being present in solid delegations. The .e•victs r• in charge of the Mon. Illy Canon Bryan of the Girden City C ithedral 4.aciated. His woida were must apiroprively tender and comf3rt- ing. The Masonic vitualistic service followed. The fbral decorstions were very profuse. The walls,l the manrelpirce and tne ca-ket were banked with ' -tutiinl and fragrant tribatcts of loving remem- btancos M isic was furnish. d by the ve.te I choir of thirty from the Cathedra' at Berrien City. ,everal Isci ins were rendered anl the tetvel,it melody of the carefully tia•nod voices added greatly to the impressive beau' y f the oc:taiiion. There was no iniermenr, the !imolas being being token at 11 C'E lick this morning to Irre4l Poni Crematory, where in accord ,nne with the last wishes of the deceased, they were incinerated."</p>
