      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>BETTER BE SAFE THAN SORRY.</head>
        <p>BETTER BE SAFE THAN SORRY.</p>
        <p>In the study of human affairs around us, offering the 'eery greatest diverefty of circumstances relating to physical troubles and sufferkg, thew is always the temptation ta say that —luck or chance baa much to do with such matters. On the other hand ,if Ain gee a MSS, bale and bratty, and well up in years, we say "He wears well,' little thinking of the carefulness, the watchfulness, and the adapting of means to an end, that such a man exercises over himself. Then, again, WC may hear of some wonderful cure effected, at tritlkne when the patient's case seemed h.-peless,how inerrilluMW we often are at such times, and if overtaken by irneel -.e are apt to go on in the old humdrum way, just be- cause of our unbelief,but depend upon it, you had ber.tet be safe than son?, iiad if you are suffering from general . .</p>
        <p>weakness, nervori;ness. arid prostration, tion to work, -without being downright i, the safest, the surest.aad the most satisfactory mriedy is "Gwil,rl Evans' " Quinine Bitters. Its wonderful powers have peeved marvellous in hundreds of cases. When purchas- ing be sure you find the name" Gwilym Evans on thet label, stamp, and bottle. without which none are genuine. sold in bottles 2s 9d and 4s 61, er in cases rf three 43 6d bottles for 123 61, of all Patent 131-clieinl Vendors and Chemists, or direct, earriaee free. from Vic Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, L,mittd, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
        <p>Ia i! called him as a witnees.—The boy stated that they st ere not playing football when the efficer came up. A Ilse had been kicked in the nose and that stopped play, tue,hter.) Witness fell into the hands of the police coestable. Previously all the boys were playing at the game eacept Caddick.—P.C. Bennett said he watched the boys for eve minutes before he went to them —Furthee questioned by Mr Steel, Leigh said that Kettle was acting as referee. (Laughter.)— The boys Clare and Bowers took part in the eames but before the arrival of the police officer Mr Clare called his a m in—The Chief Constable said Mr Clare sent a most indignant message, and he was so impressed by the bitterness of his style that be despatched an officer en a bicycle. It Was s " Bo9rtish " trick for Clare to take his son off the field. (Laughter.)—Leigh contenu- lug his evidence, said in answer to Mr Steel, that Clare eccharaged them to continue the game although he had Rent for the police.—Mr Steel submitted that there was no case against Kettle, because he was acting as referee, out the magistrates held the opinion that by officiating is tnat capacity Kottle was taking part in the game.— T e case against Caddeck was here withdrawn.—Mr Street, for the defence, said that Clare encouraged the heals and had the meanness to telephone to the police, . he withdrew his boy before the officer eame.—The Cnief Constable : If peeple will come forward and give me a statement in writing I will summon Clare and B. were upon their evideabe.—Mr Steel said the Bench would admit that boys must play at some time, and football was the prevailing game. What was wanted ass open spaces. He suggested to the Bench that if they refus d to convict the boys in this care it would bring the Corporation to a ewe of their reeponsibalicy in the m Ater aad they would have to provide open spates for these boys.—The Chairman We hsve nothing to do with the Corporation.—Mr. Stosl said he wished to make the suggestion hat the Bench should refuse to convict: The Chief I Constable was constantly summoning boys and the Beech were oonstantly convicting. The responsibility rested with the Corporation. As the hackneyed phrase had it "Boys will be boys." Be did not advocate for a t moment foothall in the streets,, but be contended that 1 the present eerie was rather hard upon the parents of the boys as they could Oct keep them in the house. The Chairman: We recognize that this football in the streets is a growiag evil, and we shall have to put a stop to it somehow. There is an justificaimi whatever I r boys playing in the streets because there are plenty ef open spaces in the town. It is all very well to say that "boys will be bays," but they have to be controlled ni lam afraid that in the present generation there is more licence growing than ought to be the case. So far as we are ce ncerred we shall have to puts stop to the mateer. We shall fine each of the ten boys se. A WARRINGTON WOMAN'S THEPT.—WEtL KNOWN CHAR. ACTER Seer To GAOL —SILVER-SW/ARM IN AN UPROAR At the Warriogton Police Court on Wednesday, befogs Mr Geddes (in the chair), and Alderman Heaton, alerearet Flan. aghao, Silver- quart, was charged with se &amp;dine a quantity of timber, of the value of 10e, the property of George Wallington, builder, Firtb-place, Froehall-lane.—Fiem the Chief Constable's statement of e facts of the case it appeared that the Silver-street Wes'eyan schools were undergoing extensions. and other bedding op, rations were being carried on in the neigh- houthood and that explained why the timber was there. It was used for making scaffolding. It appeared that ! being in the neighbourhood of Silver-street the wood had either to he chained up, Or would be lost altogether. The inhabitants thought that if anything was left lying about it was a perfectly bonaide reason by they should c 'Try it off. Prisoner wets eeen on the school pre- ; rte-es on Tuesday by Mr Harold Wallington, brother to Mr G Wallington, and was seen to take r tin two planks and carry them away. Mr Wallir g on was on the scaffolding at the time, and shoutea to her. N ) notice Was taken, and the prisoner allted off with the wood. Information was given to the police.—Superintendent Memaehan visited Silver- rare and found the via le of the timber at Flannat han's It use. Some of it was concealed uhder the staircase, and some in the close t. Mr Wallington and Mr Fair- cleueh (another bui der), had complained from day to day about wholestee depredation- and stealing of timber. Inspector Hackett had seen the woman chopping fire. wood. Whet the nfficer went to the woman's house, her conduct was such that she " roused the whole Id ' Silverequare Superintendeat Monaghan ea:d hi, hid rioter seen it in such a state before in his life. Be (the Chief Constable) said that that was rather " a tall order." but the Superintendent assured him that it was true —Prisms!, woo made her 25th appearance, was eommitted to gaol for three months with bard labour. A WARRINGTON BOY ROES His EMPLOYFR. A boy flamed &amp;mute listener, emcee address was given as Railway Square, Warrington, appeared before the magistrates at the County Petty Sessi ma on Wed - nesdeye on the charge of having on the 16. h March. eat Poulton, ernto vied the sum of 2 t 3d, remised by him on account of Wiliam .1 Shsw.—fie pleaded guilty.— Mr Shaw, who, carries on the trade of a botcher, at Poulton stated that he engaged the prisoner as his errand I,nly on the 15th March. On the day following he !twee him some money to purchase clothine. Later on he was sent with some meat to a customer and was paid 24 31, which he put in his p mket, and went away, leaving his hardest on the road in charge of another boy. Mr Shaw arrested him !se the Central Station on Monday last and took him to GI: Borough Police Office.—Mary Hamill fetid she was the mahageress of the shop in question, and on the 16,h March, the lad was sent to Mr William Joheson's at Poulton, with 3 b of mutton. He did not return.—Al!ce Tudor said she resided with her uncle, Mr Jehnson. She rpcei rid the meat and paid the. arianner 2, 31 for it.— 'The Bsnch ordered him to receive twelve etelres of the Aileen red.—The Chairman And I hope they will be vete 'OW infletted and he the means of making you a bettor 15sy.</p>
