      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>MINOR OASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>for being drunk in charge.-7 tie Chairman said that if defen dent went again to a wedding he advised him to Wtille hotne and not take a horse and eart.—The Bench Indicted a fine of Us and costs, which was immediately paid. Coos WITHOUT LICEIVOC9,— the following wrsons , on the evidence of F. 3. Bell, were fined 7s 6d each for keeping doge without licenses on the Bth inet —William Tickle, 37, Frederick-street ; John Borne, 21, Frederick-street ; Michael Foy. 17, Frederiel-street. (EIMaLr ott FIFIE.—For allowing his chimney to be on are on the 3rd inst. aeorge Cartwright, 19, Bilward-street, was fined le.</p>
        <p>TtreAneY—Before Aldermen Shaw Green (In the chair), and</p>
        <p>uutabinion and Dr. Fox.</p>
        <p>DRUNK IN CH:Worn.Thn the evidence of P.C. Woods. Thomas Kelly was fined 55 and costs for being drunk in charge of a horse and cart in Liverpool-road at 2.45 ors Mon- day afternoon. WANTED TO 811 LOCKED PP —A POLICE EN/ICHEAWIIi NAP.! ROW ESCAPS.-4Apati Quigley was charged with committing wilful damage to a plate glass window at the Police Stetion to the amount of 30s at 10.15 the previous night.—P.S. WiUismson stated that he was on duty ,in the Charge Offices when soddenly there was a great smash sad some pieces of glass fell on the desk. Wit- . nes, saw that the window just above the desk had teen smashed and went outside to look for the culprit. Quigley came to him and said" It's me, Wilibunson. I want to get 100 ed up.' He found that prisoner had broken the window with his boot. Quigley was under the influence of drink. A second or two previously witness 141111 working at the desk, just under the window, and had he been doing so when the window was broken, he would have been struck In the face with the glass. Prisoner was an habitual professional that. having been convicted 15 times. He was • thorcughly desperate character.—Prisoner, who offered i. o explanation of hie conduct. ass sent to gaol for two months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Metiers Henry Reynolds and J Ocklestoe. EDUCATION QADNI.—The following parents were hoed Si and 4s 6d costs for breach of the Ma:location .tot —WU lam 'Thomas Wood. Robert-street; William Twigg. Halton-road; (two cases) ; Willi sns Martin, Gilbert-street; and Sarah Lea. Gilbert-street; and a school attendance order was made wpon James Clarke, Okel-street. Before Mes•rt J OCkleston. and B Unison. AvTENnergis VlClDE.—Slargaret Derbyshire. the young girl remanded last week on a °huge of attempted suicide, was again before the court.—Superiutendent fl Pearson in, mated that he had been ha communication with Miss Wright of Chestkr, an he believed that Miss Wright was prepared to do what she oould for the girl.—Miss Wright said that ate had had an in'erview with the girl whilst in prison, and thought that something could be done for her it the magis- trates would at ow her to proceed to the Home at Chester:— Prisoner expressed her willingness to go. and the Bench dis- missed the case. I t CULL TB ING.—Mary Woolgar. of Bruns wiek-street, pleaded guilty to a charge of obscene langnage.—lt appeared the ease came:on for hearing last Monday, when the defend ant asked that It might be adj -turned fur her to procure w tnesses. She new said that her witnesses would not come without being summoned n.l that she tuaci not the means to do that.—Sergeant Gregory was about to prove the case when the Chairman remarked. '6 She pleads guilty. I sup- pose It's the usual thing —Deforvtant was toed 19t or 14 cloys —Lucy Newport, of Branswiok-street for a similar offence was fined 65 6cl. _ _</p>
        <p>A WoMc win( a RECORD.—Ann Carooran, a well-known oh rooter of Fryer-street,was charged In custody. with being drunk and disorAerly. and wUt) wilfully damaging a window of the Friendship Tavern La the extent of 4, 61 on the previous Saturday night.—She pleaded guilty to both charges. nowt the evid.inee of Mr Boone the lan tiord of 'he Friendship Tavern and Constables Bowyer it was shown that the woman was very drunk mot disorderly in the vicinity ' f the ' Frie dship," and besamse th) landlord refu ad to re, rye her she broke the bar window. Const ,ble Bowyer locktd her up —There was a record of 32 convictions against the prig mar --Fined 5s and Si costs for the first &amp;Merton, and be and the damage 4s 6i in the second, with an alternative of 14 days. . _</p>
        <p>A SA'n CASE.—Rdwin Parkinson plealed guilty to sleeping out on the 4unday morning previous.—P.O 01lpha proves the ease. Prisoner was found by him at abotd LiC in the morning in a stable belonging to the Manchester Shlo Canal Co.. an as be could give to satisfactory atcount or himseif he was locked up The prisoner was formerly in the employ cf the Manchester Ship Canal Co . as a driver, and had been prcreeded against at Quarter Sessions for inflicting bodily htrm on a lad.—The Superintendent stated prhrner had also keen convicted for sleeping out and fined frt. He *AM for time to pay, but as the period had expired *he'rt WAS a commitment for seven days to be executed satinet him.— Prisoner said that he had been trying to net a week in hold from the people he now worked for so that tr.• might meet the case„—The Bauch committed the prisoner for a month in addition to the week already referred to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>ODOROUS LIVERPOOL.</head>
        <p>ODOROUS LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>Quite Time for an Improvement.</p>
        <p>Much could be written as to the influeime of odours on mind and body, and to city dwellers the subj.ct is of much importance, considering the extraordinary variety of perfumes, mostly obnoxious, that excite the nasal organs of townfolk. By some the powerful aroma. of a gas wOrks is supposed to be beneficial in some ob- scure manner, but by others vigorous opinions to the contrary hive been expressed. It seems reasonable that one should object to an almost constant reminder by odour of the presence of a great industry in the vicinity and one person in Liverpool has certainly good cause for giving publicity to an opinion on this subject. Mrs Z )ea Parry is a young married lady, now living at 8, Rihblesdale avenue, Aintree, but formerlr she resided in Bootle in the immediate neighb"urhoed of a gasworks. The powerful smells arising from the gasworks. re- marked Mrs Parry to a reporter interviewing her, "seemed to upset my appetite and make me feel ill gen-ondly. I had constant nausea, and lost all colour in my cheeks,while I was very weak bodily. I saw a doctor and he told me that I was very anvemic, and that my blood was watery. This state made itmelf felt by my excessive weakness and lack of interest in life. At last I fell so weak that I had to keep to my bed for a fort- night. Doctors' medicine seemed useless. I could keep foed on my stomach, and felt faint in the morning with pains all over my body and limbs. A stomnd time I bad to take to bed then I want to Boylake fora change for some weeks, but all this time I was trying different medicines without any change for the better. I had wines of all kinds,prepared foods, eiff rent waters and all manner of. 'hinge. For foureen months I was seriouals. ill. Besides pain and vomiting, I bad noises in my head all day, until I was almost daz .d." " You must have altered greatly for the better since then," was the reporter's comment. "In fact, I have to congratulate you on your appearance." My friends far the same. When I was away in Boylake ever so many people recommended me to try Dr. Williams' pink pills for pato people, and eventually I did mo. Just after Whit-week last reit. I bought a box of Dr. Williams' pink pills, andi had taken only half the contents when quite a-change - came over ma. I got stronger every day. I ate better and digested my food easily, and my spirits CAMP back again. I kept on with. Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, and now I feel unite my former self again. Dr.. Williams' pink pills have cured me, though every other medicine tried teemed ro have no effect at all on my complaint." . " And do you give your permission to publish this account ?" " Y a," Mrs Parry replied, " I don't mind, and von may publish what I have said, and I hope that every woman who is led by my words to seek the same means of relief will profit as greatly as I have done."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>STRIKES AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>STRIKES AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>--7</p>
        <p>The cabinet makers, carvers, and rnac`winist3 in the empl iy of liessre Robert Garnett tied Sons and Greenhalgh and Company, have cline out on st,ike the masters having refused to grant them some altertrione in the werkintt conditions. Thirty-three men are affec,ed by the dis- pute'. AJ yet, no meeting between the empleyers and men has been arranged, but it is net altici- peted that the strike will continue long, both firms having a pressure of business in different parts of the country. Last week the basketmakers of Warrington ceased work, owing to the refuse' of the masters to give them An advance of wages. There are over forty men affected. It has been reported that one of the masters contemplated a reduction of the wages, but we are informed that this is un- true, as is also a further statement that the matt- ' ters have refused to meet the delegates ef the men ' to consider the qusetion and have it settled by ar- bitration, Alderman Hutchinson,onA of the masters, has expressedlhis willingness to mept, ar,• men, and to do what he can to settle the etrike, which is not regarded as very d serious.Amee effect gbt n between tie maeters an men will probably settlement.</p>
