      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>SEQUEL TO AN EJECTION.</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO AN EJECTION.</p>
        <p>Damages against a Warringtm Barman. A Dislocated Shoulder,</p>
        <p>John Ildnagrove Taylor, a to e toter, living at 551 Wattirs-a rent, Warringtonovas the ni.intiff in•n acti,n at the Warrington C:unty Court on l'hursday, last week before Ma Honour Ju fee Bowen Rowl in ,s, in svoitth he clairnd LW dainuea for personal injories sustained whilst betog ejected by Charles B. Alsn ,a barovanfrotn the licenaed prem:ses of J tseph 13.t0k,r.1, it the Hawthorn (Mord-lane, on Christmas D .y, I9oo.—Mr Thomas S Steel represented the pl.initiff and Mr Arthur Browne appeared for the defendants (Iniepo Buckard and Cnarles Bradley). Toe facts of the case as stated by Mr Steel were to the effect that the plaintiff had b .en a customer at the Hawthorn Inn f 'me enrisi I. rattle ti no. Oa Christmas Day a cousin of the plaint,ff WA4 tieing married and previous to the wedding the plaintiff and a number of friends from Salford went to the publin house in question and had some drit.ll, in the pasloor. They were about to leave when some more friends of Taylor came in and the plaintiff went back aid had a. drink with thorn. Same dispute are abut payment,plain•iff stating that be had given Bradley 4 shilline.whiCh eta e tient Bradley contradicted. A,ter some wo us had passed betw?en them, Bradley said to plaintiff. "If you don't shut up I'll throw you out." ?knit if replied, " I don't require throwing e.ut ; I can leave without that " Bradley put down a tray he Was carrying, got hold of the plaintiff by the coat and trousers and gave him a push towards ths dc,or. Plaintiff at the time had his hands in his pockets and was unable to save tintself with the result hat he was thrown agaii.st the ...oft; of the fire-place. His shoulder was so badly injored that h, was at cmcn taken to the Infirmary where his injories were attended by Dr Battle. It was found that tho man's shoulder had b 'en pu out and he was unable to follow his employ meat for five weeks.. . _</p>
        <p>ail 11-n:tour remarked frnm he facts otated by lYfi Steel he lII4W no ease against BmkAril. Mr Browne : RA was not there at all.</p>
        <p>His Honour : Sever mind wte her he was there or teet. ' If s subordinate actr fairly ami reasonably within the seope of his auvtority in the performance of his duties his moter is liable, but whert a man exceeds his duty and does not act in accordance with his authority he is liable and the master is not, Mr Sreet contended that it was the barman's duty to maintain order, ant if 4 man was divot. ierly— Ilia Honour Y u den'c all ge diaerder. Mr Browne my point will be that there was disorder. Het Honour : So far there is ne cave against the defendant Beekard. Plaintiff then gave evidence in support of his solicitor's statement. In croae-exami atiott by Mr Browne he ad. mitted that he had had the 941110 shoulder put nut three times before. Be did net use obseene or filthy lan- guage in the house, nor did he create an u seen" y die- , turbance. The barman said he would throw him out, but plaintiff had done nothing wrong. William Henry Goul ling corroherated. Be never heard Taylor make use of bad language. Taylor did not size Bradley by the watatcoat collar before Bradley attempted to put him out. Dot`, men were sober. _ .</p>
        <p>James Henry Mack, labourer, 20, Foundry-atrest, and Thomas Forbes gave videneo to the same ffect. George Taylor, father of the p'aintiff stated that on the Friday following the oc 'utrer.oe, he had a conversae cation with Bradley, who stated that he was very entry the affair had happened and asked him to see him again on Sunday. Witness went on Monday and Bradley said he "was in a fix." He n• xri rddea that he bad plenty of witnesses to say that Taylor fell outdid e. Thomas Kaye corroborated. For the defence, James Kaye, a fitter, residing in Orford-lane. stated that he was in the public house when a dispute arose between Goulding and Bradley about some change. Taylor interfered; making use an ob- scene expression, and Bradley told him that if he did not mind his own business he would pat. birn outside. Taylor again made use of ohmetio language and s Bradley by the waistcoat. Bra Bev pot down his tray, and walked Taylor to the door, where he brake away fram him and fell down exelaiming, "Oh my shoulder's gone." Bradley did not push him down and did not make use of any more violence than was necesarry to ' eject him from the house. John Cheadle, greengrocer, Orford.lane. corroborated. Charles Bradley, the defendan,, was called and denied throwing the plaintiff down. H, alto corroborated the statement that plaintiff used obscene language. Mr Browne addressed the court and s int that if the police had come in and heard the plaintiff r, peatedly making use of bad language the license&amp; would have been liable to be summoned for permitting disorderly behaviour to take place on the p-etnises. Plaintiff alBo got hold of Bradley by the waistcoat, He submitted that Bradley was justified in trying to put the man out and would twice negl-oted his duty if he had not done co. Unnecessary violoncta was not used and if that was the case the laadlord could not be held liable. Plaint ,ff did not make any allegation against Buck arl. M Browns also pointed out that Taylor had had his shravder rust out three times before and the accident wruld not have happened only for plaintiff's conduct. His Honour was r f the opinion that Taylor ibad not succeeded in his action agaiyst fiaackbax I but be had succeeded in'his claim againat Wadley. He would give judgment for Buckhard without (mats, With reoard to the case agaiw.t Bradley, Taylor might have done unwisely in insisting so strenuously,. upon the fact that be had given a shiliing to Bradlew add it might be said that Bradley had a right to expel hlth. He (his Honour) was of the oplsion that however nowise the conduct of the plaintiff might have been i' d d not amount to justification for expellidg him. Na r,quiatt to Taylor to go out fad been proved That 13,a.ttey was the first person to get hold of the other he (his Honour) had in ' his mind no &amp;Lint. He could not believe that Bradley walked Taylor as gently as nurSa would take a delicate child for an airing in the park, row.rds the door. Ir, was utterly beyond all human nature to suppose that an angry potman who had been accused of stealing a shilling and was shocked by boa i .g language which he , did not care to hear iia a public h btmo would handle him so gently. He gave judgment az,ainst Bradley for £6 and couts. The assault was abadutely unprovoked and unjustifiable.</p>
