      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>WARRINGTON BANKRUPTCY COURT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BANKRUPTCY COURT.</p>
        <p>FRTDlV—Before Mr. Registrar Ris</p>
        <p>FAILUPE or Ha NIARTM FLANAbIAN, —Martin Flanagan. Of lst. Or wd-laue, Warrington, A4r01.!'•1" ant prOViiio d-aler, pared for his artistic examination in bankruptcy. The date of the receivinr 'order was 1-,t April 1911, and the bankrupt's statement of affairs Sh 4P/Nd liabilities emanating to 23i 14s and the a—ests were estimated to produce £23 ss, leaving a deffidency of £42 93 The causes of failure as a:ltged by hankrupt were: Insuffi tlest trade bad &amp;lats, and being out of employment." The Official Receiver. In hit observations, state i that the bankrupt commenced business on Bth day. 1899 with £45 free capital ; and that his wife manag ,d the business whilst he was working as a foreman iron grinder Not hav- ing kept proper b toks of '4ce.olint (particularly neither cash boll/ nor creditors ledger) he could not, file the prescribed deaden:ls Recount, boat attribute I his d ficiency mainly to hou ehold and personal expenditurein excsss of income. He only first became aware of this insn'vanay about a month ago when he tell out of work. He had not since contracted any (Mats now owl ig, end on the 3 lth ult., sold nearly the whole el the hou &amp;told furniture to a broker for £1.5 7s 69. and paid the Court £l4 to his solicitor towards the costs of filing his petition and balanc3 towards othar costs. The unsecured liabilities ware for : Trade goots. £46 2s ficl olothing ; rent LW 10s ; and medical attendance 2e. he examination wee clOsed.</p>
        <p>A ICUNIoRS TAILOR'S PAILURE.—John Pox, of 66, High. street, Runcorn, carrying on business as a tailor and out. fitter. under the style of Fox Brothers, was publicly examined by Mr Johnson (assistant edict .1 receiver), the gross liabilities are expected to rink £1,184 8s lid and the assets after deducting preferential creditors to £583 18s 4d leaving a defielency of £6 /0 +Os 71, The bankrupt alleged as the cause of failure " bad debts " Tee official receives its his observations, reported that the bankrupt stated h. ninenced business in May. 1893. with £75 borrow6d from b., father. He accounts for his dernolency as fallows :—liebi.ities excess of assets on 18th April 1900' £l5O : bad debts. £4B Is 110 ; anticipated loss on realisation of assets, £405 13s ld ; law cost., £5 ; and household and persomal expenses in excess of profits sine° 18th April, 1900, £9l lOs 7d. He has not, kept any cash book, b,it has kept a daily record of his cash takings and debtors' aol creditors' ledgers, and subaieiary books but which have never been balanced, and to not, at any time, disclose his financial post Mon. About three months ago he prepared a statement of his assets and liabilities, but with what result he could not at present say. He only first became aware of his insolvency "early lest month" [March], and has since, practically. eon. tracted no debt no a owing The ten esured liabilities are for ; —Trade goods. £666 10s 91 money teat by father £503 ; and work done, rode ,rtiainit. gas, end c sel. £l4 18s 2d. The bankrupt was examined at nonsiderable length by Mr Johnson (issistint official receiver) who told him that he had been wilfully blind to his true position and had not taken steps to see what position he was in. The creditors refined the 00 npositioa he eff emii them because they wished to investigate the claim: of his father. —The examination was adjourned to June 7th. FAILURE td, a. NEWrON PURLICAN Whitley Owen, residing and eerrying on business as a licensed victualler at the Millstone Hotel, Newton-its-Willows was publicly examined in bankruptcy. The gross liabilities Were expected to rank £469 '2s 4d and the ass.ts.after deducting Claims of nreferant lel creditors. £29 12s, leaving a delloieney. of £440 Os 4d. The causes of failure as alleged by the bankrupt were " low turtsover,high rental, a large family " Prom the official receiver's observations it appears that the bankrupt commenced business at the Millstone Hotel in September. 1898 Not having kept proper books of amount, (particularly neither cassia book nor ledgers) he onull not file prescribed di ficiency a-count. but attributes his deFicilency to the hiah rental of the hatel and to household and personal expenditure in excess of income or profits. He first became aware o his insolvency ahout 12 months ago; and has sines, contracted debts now owing. The unsecured liabilities, are for trade goods, £205 Its 4d ; money lent and interest, £152 ; gas and water £lO 3s ; and medisal attendance. £1 153.—The examination was closed. AN EARLEsToWN PLUMBER'S SECOND PAILLTRE.—Thornas Waring, ni 14 Oxford-street Rsrlestown, a plumber, also appeared for his public examination in bankruptcy. The gross liabilities were expected to rank £389 Os 5d and his assets, after dedursting the amount due to preferential credit- ' ors, £52 5s 4d leaving a deficiency of £337 Os Li. The causes of fame as alleged by bankrupt were " Lessee on eontreets; Insufficient trade " In his observations the Official Receiver reported that the ban'arront, commenced his present Sogginess about nine years aga without capital : and was adjudged bankrupt on lfah August 1888. theassets yield ingt dividend of 3s 7.1.1 on liabilities for £304 9s 3d. He obtained his di.- Charge on the 15th November. 1888, subject to three years suspension. He ace eras for his present deficiency as follows : —Anticipated loss on realisation of assets £6l 3s ; law eosts,£2); and household and personal expenditure in ex- cess of capital and protits,£2ss 17s id. He has kepticash book since J nuary 1398 (but never balanced), and debtors' and creditors' ledgers, and subsidiary books, but has not. during the pastilthree years, ascertains his financial position. He only Mot became aware of his iniolveney about, two months ago and his since contraated some .mall debts now owing. we alleges that he lass lost from £303 to £4OO on contracts ' during the past three years. His wife claims the household furniture. and a palicy for £231. issued by the New York Mutual Life Assurance Company, the furniture having been purchased out of her savings from letting apartments, and gifts front her parents. The unsecured liabilities are for:— trade got' '4, 91)9 ¶'). ; money lent, Ac. £53 10s; sundries £63..-- x was closed.</p>
