      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS. Mounted Rifles Patrol Out Up.</p>
        <p>CR&amp;DOCK, Wednesday.</p>
        <p>A P itrol of 30 men of th Mtc,potarl !daunted Rifles lost bur kdial and sic yrnided in a skirmish near Bilraisburgt4(C4pe Colony), yeaterd y. Other parts of the chstriv: are a". present quiet.</p>
        <p>De Wet Reported in Cape</p>
        <p>Colony. Another Invasion.</p>
        <p>ROS3fE&amp;D, Wed flesiday.</p>
        <p>Uis reported, thou;ti unofficially, that Da Wet has again crossed into Cape C dotty, and is in the Zattrberg. with a Loag Tom. Small putties of B :erm are reappearing in this district.</p>
        <p>Neetrweoon, Wedneqd‘y.</p>
        <p>new commando, unier Lategun,, a C)losberg rebel, crossed into Cap.l Colony a few days ago. It is a hundred strong.</p>
        <p>Departure of Liverpool Volunteers from Warrington. The members of the Liverponi Speial Snatice Corn- who have been for an iut a month in training for service in South Africa, hive at length, after several postponeatents,left Wsrring,no on tneir W,7 tn 014 front They paraded in Orford 134rrack4 shortly after 10 min, on Monday, and sub ~giently in irched to Bulk Q ity Station, udder the cornmarni of Captain Hign,•tc, by the usual route /Ili-mg Orfoi d-1 ne. Tanners-lane, Frogball-lane, Crosfield.4trt and Parker-street, and left for London by the 11.5 train. Toe men, who num- bered 110, were in high spirits, and tnieir departure was witneised by a large cr intd of people, who gave them a most enthusiastic send-off. The hr ur was much earlier than has uiu illy been the case, and this enabled a num- ber of friends of the departing Vdu iteers to bid fare- well to them on the station itself, consequently the platform of the sta -sented a very animated appearance as the tr.in left the station. The Liverpool V ilunteera go ou in the (Meat, which Sailed from Seudismoton on Weelnoad oy afternoon br South Africa, having ecnarkei 31 offi ;Pr; and 661 men, the priecipal draft b log 400 Soutn African C oustabu- lary. There are also 107 men of the Scottish Volunteer Cyclist Company.</p>
        <p>Honouring the Runcorn Volunteers. The workmen of the" Sprinch Yard "(M s C. C 0..) on Tuesday evening last en'ertained the retu.ned Special Service Volunteer., Sergeant Jeacock. Lance-Corporal Speakrean,and Privates Mack and Piatt, to a haeq Jet at the Royal Hotel. Runcorn. There were about 70 eovers and the guests included Mr Jeaeock, swior. et Ef slton. sloe father of the popular sergeant. The catering was carried out in Mr A Raining's bee': style, and was • praised on all hands. After the barsq let Mr Dale or, sided, and on behalf of his fellow workmen of the Sorinch Yard, extended a hearty welcome to their gueits, and gave expression to the pleasure all felt at ih iir safe retuim. The toasts Which followed included : "The It )tral "The Arm., Navy, and Antifiary F item" Co which Sergeant Jeacock and Lance-Corporal Speakman responded), and one "That the war woe' be brought to a epee iy and sncoessfut iasue." All four " guess" gate short addresses, and sem ,ts of their exp*riences and also expressed their appreciation of their comrades kind hospitality.--Songe were rendered by Messrs W Shaw, T Careens, C Leathwood, W Pen Rebury. an i A Ravens- croft, and a capit.l tisa hones jurslery entertainment ALI given by Mr J Clare.—Mr WILU&amp;LXI Le ithweed acted as raccompanist.—lt is worthy of note that the whole affair was promoted and catered for tov workmen of the yard—the Bpriecti-- which boa done so much on many an occasion to gladden and hritzeiten the town. Vote, of thanks to the Ch airman and the*' Host " for bill excellent catering were prop )sed by Mr Ira Withing- ton, and seconded by ter Holt, sod u mnim lush; carried. —Expression was also empharically gives in favour of the Volunteers heing provictid with some lasing memento of the town'a gratitode for their unselfish</p>
        <p>services. The Frodsham Volunteers. Presentation of Watches. Last Saturday evening a public meting was held in the Town Elan, Frodsham, for the purp me of milting a presented mii the six V duriteers will have recently returned from active errvice in 9 uPi Africa. There was a large audience, and mile. enthusiasm wal dis- played during ,he prleeedin74. Th. Chai^man of the Parish Counoil(Mr Charles E Li eaker)presidedoind by his Side were Mrs Garratt,th. Re R H Biogg(c'oaplain of the Company), ild•j)r Ra, Rrriso, (commlnding officer), Captains Bnrton, Ashton an iTimmlns, Lieuten- ant Bell and W G Linak irman said the Volueteers had had a hard rime, but notwithstanding that be was quite sure that if the country flooded their myervices on a future occasion every one a them would respond to the call. The gifts, which were to be bestowed were intended as a memento es their South African experience, and h. believed each one would be highly prtsd. (Chf era.) He called upon Mrs Garrett to preKent the watches in the name of the inhabitants of Frolshara. (Cheer.) Mrs Garrett said she was well assured the watches would be an acceptable present. The V dunteers had acted very nohIT in going out, and the couetry had great nnefiience in the men who had rallied so nobly round tha ,Id flag to do their duty. (Cheers.) Mrs Garrett then handed a neat silver lever watch, suitahly ioscrin-d nu the inner case to Sergeant John Holland, Lance-Sergeant George Rolgers, Corporal L Calvelny, Lace s-Crrporal F Evans, Priv ites E Dariington and T Carter. Mai r Harrison, on behalf of the Volunteers, thanked the innahiqacits for subscrib- ing to the presents. A vote of thanks was passed to Mrs Garrott. Later Mejor Harris.), entertained flys officers and Voluoteers in the Drill Hall. Dun i,l an interval he, on behalf of his siife,wesented each of t'oe six active Fervice Volunteers with a silver chain to at-ac, to %vie pres.n- Winn watches. On the Sunday morning following the V )lunteers,under the command of Mai ar Harry 11 arri.on, at ended the service at the Parish Church. Fee' was A targ. con- gregation. The Tear, the ft ur fl B B ,g preached. pH4!</p>
