      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>DECLINE OF THE • ENG'_ISH VILLAGE. Rural England and the Census.</head>
        <p>THE DECLINE OF THE • ENG'_ISH VILLAGE. Rural England and the Census.</p>
        <p>(By Sampson Morgan.)</p>
        <p>It is evident trnm tis.• Ceesiall r urns that have be-n published that the rural reputations continue to decline, and that in spite of the b oa.ted enactatente that have been pas,ed, presumably for the beeefit of tenant culti. vetoes, things in the rural districts have erne from bad to worse. It matters I trees in which direction we torn, whether to Huntingdonshire, Canal-wider ishiee, or Bed- fordshire, the rosults are pretty nearly rite same. Eat n in Kit, justly termed the Garden of &amp;misted, out of ten of the brst agricultural districts nine show a decrease in population, whilst in the remaining one the figures rand the same as they were ten years bark. Buret E .gland has been upon the decline, as far as p .pulation is concerned, for yearsond though we posses tho fi iest mork rs in the werld, the richest soil, and the most congenial climete for fruit and profitable garden crop% yet the English markets have been invad d by the foreign producers, wile he result that 'hey take ett of the United IT,nedorn some- thine like £150.000 000 for general food produeta, one, half at least of whiou tatht cosily be kept at home. Whilst we are opening up new markets abroad we are losing those we possess at hemp. We have leas to fear from the f +reign bayonet tea,: we have from the foreign spade, for by the triumph nf the latter we are miectset by our victors of an indemnity of £75,000,000 per annum at least. What is the real cause of the trouble? Why do's the Ene• lish peasant forsake the fields of his father and wend his way to the ov-rerewded t )wn ? " Bee twe of the dul- ness of his country life," is the reJly, act social reformers are assured that SO long as country lin is shorn of the gay attractions wnicri ft unt ti e ngelvea in the highways of the hu4thrigri long will the rural exodus continue, though a enu-co of national weakness. This impression has unforlu la'ely become general. Is speaking of the aseiestions of the rural pepulation the cynical Salisbury *ski, " Parish Councils, indeed! They do not want Parieh 0-sioncies; giv them a eircus." CITY LIFE AND MORTALITY. The growth of city life, owl matt), with the decrease in rural populations, is, en my mind, nothing less than a national calamity.. Lord Beab,a in. with regard to cite life, has side that " things teing equal, the rate of mortality is in direct pr spertioo Cot le density of popu'- atien, or, in other words, the nearer pessole live to each other the shorter their lives." If, then, the land is net properly cultivated, then the rural populations will be eltiven to the towns, and the trading community bs heavily bureened with increasing tax lass. Unless the agricultural and in lu.trial communities are both in a prosperous c mdition the progress of • the nation will be limited, to say the least. In the citie3 poverty is rampant, and there is a decline in mnrals, and a marked deterioratirn in the race. It is true we h ye, as the result of the develop- ment ef indastril occuoations, a centinued increase in our volume of true, hi 7. at what a cos"? I adonc e return to those coa'3i'iog under which the pro spsrity not of one bat of all diesel of people, was one) secured. At the time of Henry VIII the name of pauper Was un- known. The land Was CilliiratAd by village communi- ties, with the re-ult that all were in a prosperous condition. Te-day these village c 'immunities do not exist., SMALL HOLDINGS IND EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>I claim, first, that the want of an I qeitable measure of land law reform is ths cauae of all the trouble. I re- peat what I have stated year after year for a quarter of a century, th.t it, gm .11 hold,oge of land utileted uneer improved systemi ef culture will be found the true oolu- tion of the probl-m tf ars v.l age.; and that n 'at. to a reform of the land laws, practical eciantioe by the aid of the village scheel will ensure prosperity to rural Eng- land. F.ar the welfare of village isduitries connected with land I have always advocated the utilisation of village schools, to every one of which an allotment, or experimental garden should be att ached. I have no faith in rural lectereships. I have psrsonally watched the movement, and am satisfied that the immense sum • of money spent in this direct•on wouls he productive r f real good if it were utilised in giving practical and scientific educalinn in rural schools to those who may in future years be getting their living from the soil. There is a great want in rural di •tricts for workers with the , educit'oe referred to, besid•s which it would tend to attach tnesseaods of this portion of the community to the land, or would be helpful to those who left the old country to try their fortunes In dis'ant portions of the Empire. Rural education in village scnools would net productive of untold good in the direc' ion of up-to-late fruit aid mark it cultivation. It is a lamentable error th imagine that the land tiller or hue bondman has no need o' ()location, wad that he can bled along in the old groove as his father did before lalm. Things have chanereri. In my apieion, the horticulturist has as much need of scientific knowleige as the mechanic or professional man. The success of the Mee. try is of vital importance to the nation, and the com- munity should see that efficient instruction in every branch of land cultivation is eniuted to the village , scholar, and that an experimental garden of *mole pro.: , portions is annexed to every rural schen!, forming a valuable centre of instruction for the young and of experiment for the older members of the district. The fruit-grower, of all men, must bring scientific know- ledge and skill to hear in the cultivation of his plants and trees if he wishes to hold his own against foreign Competition. ENGLISH VILLAGE COMMUNITIES. If we rarer to the q se' I)O of tee p tor a d tithes, I believe we shall find that up to the time of toe R-f o-re.- tion there were in E igland something like 13 COO parishes, in each 'of w aich was a rector or vicar. It was the custom in those class fir thi rector to b.t kept from the tithes, and the poor were relieved out of the balance, to which they were enti-Aed by right. Thus the tithe belonging to the priest and the poor, and if any further balance remained it went towa di ksepine the parish chinch in repair and a part to the bishop. The priest took a far greater interest in the utilisation of the land than he dees at present, and this accounts for thefact that the country parishes were folly occupied by cultivators, hence the prosperous condition of the rural districts, and the absence of poverty. Historical writers testify to the beneficial eff.cts of such a system. They aver that at this time the onerch was an easy land- lord, because it was her interest to attach the popell- tion to her; hence her lands were always bit upon long lease of lives and at low reetals. As the renult, the woes of the agricultural labourer was high, and the profits of the traders ample. These facte are of vast imiortance to us. They show, beyond doubt, that where the village communi- ties cultivate the soil they are enahled to live in com- parative comfort and teas. ; that suet' districts are not , productive of paupers: and that had the same system been perpetuated to the ()resent day there would have been non ed for the deerading "Poor Law Amend- ment Act." It is evident that the pos•tion of the tenant farmers was generally all that couid be desired, and if we refer to the position of the lab urer, both in town and Country, the same stare of things will be felled to exist. Turning to Thornton, we see that married men engaged as labourers ie husbandry were proviJed with a cottage and a few acres of land to cultivate fir their own profit i.i the intervals of their master's work. SO satisfactory was the condition nf the English peasant in these days that in the veer 1349 his wa zee were con- sidered to be se excessive that , h • representative of the landowners in the House of C mem ons art lopt.d to limit them by the famous "Statue ef Labourers," which revired all servants to accept the wages which were custninsry Wit -or nine year nr.rieusly. —THE MAGIC OF OWNERSHIP. . .</p>
        <p>_ Althotlzh there hes been a good incre ise in orchard areas as the result of the frui,szrowing revival, yet it is evi !eat that the m wernitnt has been hind triad by the present inequitable sts'erns of tenure, which frustrate the development of the fruit .nd fool resources of the people at home It le clear that, before we cal extro" to see small farms springing up on every han I,growora must have that secoriry of tenure whilti wit jus ify them in improving their holdings, by sinking cipital in the land. Toe great want of the day is small holdings, rangin4 from five to twenty acr.l each obtainable at a fair agricultural rent, and (torn which the cultivator cannot unress lushly be rein wed, so tong as hie rent is paid. Uo .!Pr this system a great impetus would be given to petit cultur. i this c•amtry ;we should have a revival of village industries, arid the dis- astrous migration of rural popolations to the nye.- crowded cities and towns would soon he stayed. In , dealing with land developrtinnt, a wriri once .aid : IThere can be no doubt in the mind of anyone who has paid the least attention to the pre.ent state of things in Germany, B.lgiurn. Swi'z flan I, Franc., and the Channel blends, that the ownership of small. plots of land does in those countries give the h ipe and the energy which we so much want here ; that it can exit eid e by side with large properties and large farm+ ; that it is consistent with very ample production ; that it gives the land a very high price; and that there is nothing in the climate, th soil, or other inevitable conditions of English life which Phould pt.., int he develop- ment in England of what succeeds so well in foreign countries. These are the sentiments upon which I hasp my appeal on behalf of the fruit tension movement,with the exception that I am strongly in favour of Free Trade in land Mader this arstien a host of evils would disappear as ii 1w Made ;t' e land would be develop, d to the utmost extent of its produc- tivity, and instead of, as at pres.rit, un ler a million and a half of labourers finding occupation on the land, we should coon have openings for ten million workers at least. Our Pr'S",t system rf tenure II the great block to agricultural impravement ; it etic- urrrra im- provident s:stema of chirp,, arid, under its retarding irfluPoce it is to the interest el' the farmer simply to g.t from the eon all h° can with the liaat p,„4t.q„ „thy, and with as little labour as ptissibie, with the re6u! [hit the fertility of the la, d d.crPases from year to sear from the want of fort::eatirr.i•</p>
