      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>EXAMINER FRI )'l/4 V. MAY 17 1901. NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRI )'l/4 V. MAY 17 1901. NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>In reference to one uf the mnat productive and pros- perous dirtricts iii France, we find that all 'inland is divided into estates, each of which, aa a gmeral rule, is afloat the enz7 sufficient for maintaining a family, a ,is made to ield all that thought and lab .ur can extfacr from it. Under the system a dense pop I .tiort is co Me- ta i mid in toe surrounding districts for the wages of labour, the profits of cultivation, and the rent of the hod are all in the same ham% and are a!! spmt on the same spot. The surplus of the ereat amount of produce extracted fr im the land.by this dense agricultural p ,pue Pei in is exchanged in the surrsundiog cities or towns for manufactured goods, and is the means of maintain. inn' a correspondingly large number of pe tpie engaged in 'business, for nothi lig contributes so muth to the prosperity of the towns as their being surrounded by a community of succesaful cul-ivators. Now, I say, we have only to extend or introduce such a system into E ogland, and we should soon have a revival of those bonne and village industries which, on..acc aunt of the deoreision tha-- exis'.9 in the agriculturcl districts. are 'fa -t declining, and in many instances have ac'ciallif ceas dto exist 01 the French gardeners it is said :"They are frugal and honest to a de4r. e " They slave away from morning to night on a diet which many would luck down upon with, contempt. Wages are low, and protection sends up the prices of food and clothing to fabulous sums. N wertheless.it is quite the exception to find an agricultural labourer who has not a respectable account to h credit at the savings bank ; whilst the fact that the ....teeter portion of the rural population figur, c as peas an: prop ietors is one of the grandest safeguards that coal i passibly exist against bad condect and lawlessness. fhe min with a stake in his country, however small, bscomes inevitably a better and mere contented citizen. The French are very often misjudged. They are generally spoksn of as a careless, pleasure-loving set, whilst their spirit of economy fru- gality, and love of thrift are not unfrequently entirel, lost sight of. To their honour, let me record this fact, there is much to admire i t the conduct of the rival population. Vice amongst them is almost non-existent, whilst with trios,' exceptions whin x;it in every country en the face of toe earth, the people area polite, sober ra :Nan:tongs whom drulitenness t.s unkra4wn. I base mot Mwi fit them in their marke plac“s, aui al- ways fouad tnem courteous ti a degree.</p>
        <p>COBDRN AD UMW.. WORKERS.</p>
        <p>Richard C3-OTID -11 aII as friend of rural worivre. Were his principles accepted in their entirety more good would be done to improve the position of the lab Air— ing populations in country and townlin one year than has been done brewery c stnbinati a of workers that has arisen. In the House Of Cnnansone, on Marais 13th. 1845, Cotidea said : "If you bad abundance of capital employed on your farms, an I cultivated th soil wi the same skill that the manutacturers conduct their bust-es, yon woull not have pooulatlrin enough to cultivate Vie land " again he said : " C,pital shrinks instinctively from ',security of tenure and we have not that enmity wh eh will warrant men of capital investing thiir money in the sell*" From these wards it ia evident that tenants; acre prevente 1 from developing the land to the fullest extent of its powers, and for lack of capital the migrating labourers are unable to obtain employment eta fair wage, Will it be denied that the landlord claims and takes over improvements without piyrnen-sin E 41 .n I to- lav Weil,here us a cis. that was Avoided in the Courts withinjthe past few days. A market gardener, of Brigs!, was charged with doMg malicious d image to a glamahouse, the propene of the owners! What was he plea? lie said he had not damaged any property of his landlords. What he had damaged wee his own, for be had bsught the glasshouse and paid for It; said the jaw through its represeetatlve, " This is a very Ml ease," and the market gardener was ordered Co pay £5 fine and damages aul 7s c sate ! Yet we wonderavhy it is that rural E 1;21 and is no the decline! This is by no means an isolated case. I cpot IC because it ,a up to-data. are. GLADSTONE AND LELDSLATIvis verreas.</p>
        <p>la this corium:id t C ablea'a argument-is—COaclusive when he argues that not only does the want of security per vent capital from fl awing to the soil, but it actually hinders the improve:neat of the land by thorm wh occupy it. Tnere are many tenants will could imp-oi. their land if t9ey were male secure—they either ' have capital themselves, or th-ir friends could advance jr; but with the want of leases, with the want 1 security, they are deterred fra:n laying out their money. Tenants. therefiro, aro prevented by their landlaals from carrying on cultivation properly. Tney are made servile and dependent, I have thus made it clear that whilst advocating Free Yrade in c, ra, he at the same time urged a ben, ficent measure of Land Law reform, land wirh the possession of rquitable systems of tenure the ' . . Swish fruit grower can hold his own 11513,it1s the pro- duce,s of tha world. Or feu Wised lan I system isalmost as ha I to-day as when C mb le n uttered the historic words I have quoted. Aud it is imperative in their own interests that the rural and urban populations should combine to secure security of tenure to the tenant farmers of the 13 4ited Kingdom. The fruit growing movement I inaugurated means a rest aratioa of village communities ; it means that lab mi. eh )uld have free accoss to the land ; it means that th worker should be enabled to acquit° by legislative enactment small hold- ings of land wider equi-Able systems of tenure; it means that all restriction,' which hinder the development of the ;ti ,n's acres smuld he swept away; and that we should encourage the creation of a contented peasantry ,as the best safeguard of prosperity and peace. Tee pro- gramme received commendation in hign quarters, and in connection therewith Mr Gladrone wrote me saying, " I heartily sympathise with you in ysur desire for the emancipation of the land from legislative fetters."— Daily N</p>
        <p>Cricket. Warrington were on Sat urdey robbed of what would in all prn,ability have taq, n a handsome umtory, by Fel her Time, Bootle were their opponents, and the en- agenrot took place on the Arpey enclosure in the presence ot a fair sprinkling of sp-ctators. The Role captain won the toss, and, having doubts as to toe con- dition of the wick, decided to allow the home team to b.. first. %Carrington amassed te ub itantial total of 163 for the lose of nine wickets - when the innings wes declared closed. The time agreed upon by the teems did not allow for the conclusion of the math, end at the close of play, Bo Ale had lost eix .vick-ts for 75 runs. Had time permitted Warrington would no doubt have come out winaeta by a respec-able margin.</p>
        <p>The feature of the m atc I was the brilliant batting nf rhearlis, the home profasaional and J W Bibby, The first-^aTted player wcai in splendid form, and !bough at the c•inamencernent of hi a irillitlffS runs came very slowly, baying got properly set, he WI nut with freedom and ran up a total of 55. Barely has the profPss• ions! been seen to such advantage, hi score being com- piled with scarcely a faulty stroke. He bit seven fours and made three line boundary strokes.</p>
        <p>J. W. Bi bb y, the home cap.ain, rnade s grand display Despite the many changes of bpwling which were tried by the visitors he hit all round the wick t, and, in tb. end card •cl out his bat aft.r placing 42 to his credit. Uis vigorous bits includ Id four boundary strokes.</p>
        <p>Mention must also be made nf Hirnnion's brief but useful innings. 13 knock dup2s in eight arrniv.s. five fours, two two'3 and a singls. HIS four initial hits were all boundary strokes.</p>
        <p>Dr. Smith had the misfortu—nr to be caught out before he hid contrihutld a sine lo run. F C J-ihnson eff.ctPd tha c itch, which was an exceedingli srnart piece of work.</p>
        <p>W trrington's start waq an athing but prornisinc,,, two wicket,' belay down for ten runs: With the advent of Tnewl4 and Gibbons matters hlwever improved gre ttly. Gibbon's innings hold 0'31(70 13 when he failed to nego- tiate nee or Crouch's slows, and missing, was stumped by Jones.</p>
        <p>Callon tlok his place at thm wicket, and the total for the home side hal reached 77 when he was bowled by Smith, having contributel an acceptable 11.</p>
        <p>Thewli eMtinuni to play in capital style and was now jained by W H l'aimore, who was emelt by John- fon after making three.</p>
        <p>When the aggrftgate !rid reached 85 Thew lie was caught at the wickit by Jlnee.</p>
        <p>J %V Bibby row to-A the field and hit out in praiss worthy style. J Brigs J H nugh•on and G h n wsr,, dini4ei be two runs, and Hatrioslo j9i e i the hirns captain• Blth bstamen wsre in ti,.t rats torrn and when the total had been raised to 162 the closure was applied.</p>
        <p>-- Bantle had only tima to • save the match, and during the 90 minutes left they lost six wickets for 75 runs. Dr M cLny and J P CrOIMEI iiho wed the bast forrn,their innings realising 17 and 12 reipectiyely.</p>
        <p>Tne fiAding en blth sides ,7048 Vlfir smart. As regards the bswling, J W Smith did spleadii work f)r the visi. tirs, taking six wits at a cost of 133 runs. J P Crouch wss responsible for 3 wickets at a cost of 3.5 runs.</p>
        <p>A draw in Warringt favour was also the result of tha S icond Elovao's visit to Bootle. The silicon won the toil and batted first, and were all out for 162 H H Emus and P B Hasvtin 0..c1a rattled up 31. and i. Fairclongh compiled 20 not oat R Pierp tint's innings reaiiz 4cl 19. Bootle had raised 78 for the Ives of six wickets when the match was brought to a close. The 6 aiding of W Hind. of Warrington, is worths. of special mention, that player effecting some capital sav^s.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Milts were baaten bit KnottV ash, the maj Tity in favour of the victors bior 32. Smith for triP winner; and J Evans for the Wire Mul4 batted well knocking 17 up and 14 r.spectively JJones and Burton did good serv.ce wi;to tile bill, the f )rin.r 'kin g seven wickets for 21 runs, and the la-ter six for 23 runs.</p>
