        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>. A novel case, and one wiich is of importance to cyclists came before Judge Russell at the Wandsworth County Court on Minday. A Richt:nand solicitor, Willi tea tlamrnoul. su I a firm of Bath cycle mak -rs, Messrs Jet? and Comoany, for £2O, the value of a bicycle and personal injuries sustained by him through the breaking of the machine. The defence •was that the defendants did not guarantee the machine, that it was conatruc'ed to carry a man fourteen stone, and would do so if reasonable wear and tear were allowed His Honour thought the case came under the Sales of G mds Act, and tha question was whether the bicycle was fit to carry the plaintiff. He tnought it di I not come up to what was nece.stry, ad ha gave ju Ittmer, hr the plaintiff for the amount claimed and costs. ,Leave to appeal was granted.</p>
        <p>when Sr, Courtenay Boyle sat at the Blard A point of considerable interest to cyclists was raised on Tuesday, of Trade to hear obj CiOoS to tle nonirmitioa of the Wales and Laughton Ught Railway Ord +r, approved by the Light Railway Commissioners, for making a railway six miles long.—Mr LBlie J Williams, on behalf of the Cyclists' Touring Club, askad that the railway should be compelled te take bicycle as passengers' luggage f )r 3 1. any distance,—!r Cripps for tha promoters said no railway was limited to any charged h the e fooornt bicycles a concession for a rai way company to carry a bicycle ndet. cca by pagsenger train.—Mr vv m 'bard of Trade had power to compel the carriage for bicycles, and to fix the rates.—Sir C Boyle : If I gave you this amendment you would treat it as a thumb- screw, I must consider the matter .—The Order will be decidHl shortly by the Board of Trade:</p>
        <p>On the Crystal raise!) track on Tueselav evening Eliward best the world a record for one Milo. standiri nut. unpiced. He clvered the distance in 2min 3ion. Th- prpvioas bost undcr simnl.ir conditions was 2viin. 6 4-514ec. by J Platt-Betts on the same track in 1897.</p>
        <p>• Last Tanday 1114}7:r Ormrod, of Peckhill Hall, Ill,nbighshire, and Halliwell Hall, Lancashire, and jaatice of the peace for Plintshire arid Dmbighseire. met with a nasty accident. Cycling dlavn a steep bill near Wrexham. he passed a herd of swine, one of which colli- ded with the bicycle and pitched hi.j.ir Orcarod heavily CU to the road, breaking his opilat-bone.</p>
        <p>The la Idea death, through over-exertion by crcling, of Fred Greenwood, a Todrnorden youth, is announced. Ein had aufffred from heart diiease, and been medically attended, fle took to cycling, and after a short spin on a level road on Monday, begot off his machine, said he felt tired, and immdiately expired. Over-exertion caused a su idea failure cf the besrt's action.</p>
        <p>The Cynlists' Touring Club of France has undertaken 'to build 300 shelf ,re for the use of cyclists in the less fri,givntf d parts of the cluntry. Bich shelter will con- tain sleeping accolmlnodVion of a primitive nature, a medicine &amp;mit for injured cyclists, and a well-fined surgery for injured cycles.</p>
        <p>A Chinese lantern is not a light within the meaning of the Aet, accordisg to rount decisions of magistrates up and down the country.</p>
