      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>FRENCH PE ISANFRY.</head>
        <p>THE FRENCH PE ISANFRY.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRADES COUNCIL</p>
        <p>Resignation of the President.</p>
        <p>The Basketmakers' Strike.</p>
        <p>Is it a Case for the Conciliation Board?</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington rrades and Labour Commit /cal bald in the Du di Rai. Birikly- streel Tuetdg eat. ning Mr J Edwardson, Vice- Wedeln. preind .1 over ag34 a rice of delegates. ma CO•iCILLITION BOARD Councillor A bittnawrit. *arty* iniorming the- Cnoncil that Mr John Fairalouub bad declined to ac t as a mem- b.r of the B/ard of C ),,cmati in, voti the Coand oi toe Chamber at Cradtrierc had appointed Mr William Fier pout in his ,lace. _</p>
        <p>At 4 later stage of the meeting, the Cnairmin reported that the first meeting of the Board took p Ace law Thurs- day at the offi tes ot the Cnainber of C mtwtrce, when the rulAs M De adopte I fly th 3 Botyd str.re cua ider-d. The melting pasKii off very satisfactorily ant it wa4 probable that the Board would meet again at an earty date.</p>
        <p>Mr PODSIOI/8 remarked that a very fair minded tone prevail() i uuri.,g the whole of Erie meting, the repre• 9,•ntatives of b.(th bodies seeming t-) be equally desirous that the rirht thing should he done. Mr PLINSTON read the rules which bad been adopted I by. 00 Bo srd. _ _</p>
        <p>'lt W4S (b-cided that the vestinn of interchange of delegates lie r.n the table fnr the present.</p>
        <p>RESIGNATION Or TRU PRESIDENT. The SECRETARY ell,l ,a fever trim the President of the Couecd (M A C idiot{ ) stAting that he bid clackers? &amp;hided to re ire tern the C•eincil. Be therefore took the opportunity of offering the Council his resignatisn as president, and of thanking them for the courte,y they had shown to him daring his connection with the Council. Be forward-c 1 th•i brelaws of th. Manchester I3Jarl of Conciliation and wiihed them uvery striceas in their venture, Witch was ww:thy of all support. Mr BATTER BY asked if th v nal no more information in reepect to Mr Utak 'a resignation. The SECRETARY said Gnat was the only letter he had ' received. lie wrote back to Mr Binks expressing the hope that he had not d ñitiv decided to resign, be- cause he(Mr fiott) was of the opinion that the delegates would devl7 regret his action.</p>
        <p>Mr GRP:ALES move thal; the Councii refuse to accept the resign.rio ), and b.t a deputation bn appointed GO Visit Mr Bulks and wit him to reconsider his decirion. Mr JONES se3onded and the motion was unanimously agree I to,</p>
        <p>-The Cu'ainattas remarked that it would be a loss to the Council tf Bluks resigned. Be had keen a very worthy prehdent up to now, and he (Mr Edwardson) thoug. it that they should obtain some further 'donna- tior. in the matter.</p>
        <p>MT BATTERSBY It k a Burg:oho to me altngsther. It wag decid,a the the deputation should consist of Mes rs Pliostoci and Eiwardson had the searetary (Mr B*Ott.) WAY TES EMMET MAIM,' VITINT ing fiTßlgg —monE INF EIIiATION.—IS IT A CASIC Ve THIT CONCILIATION</p>
