      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>BOARD?</head>
        <p>BOARD?</p>
        <p>Mr G the —lll-e'CrTie-ry of the Warrington Brim+ ,ie ,h,. I..mcishire and Clreuthire Croton of Skip and Siskot Makers, had written informing the Secre- tary that the basket makers were out on strike owing to the m.stera not being willing to comply with their hat cf p i,a. . _</p>
        <p>In answer to a letter from Me &amp;-o.t, the following add:lanai communication was received .—" Lancashire and Coeshire Unice] of Skip and Bakst makers, 13. Frederick-street, Latchford, May 7th, D .ar Br.—ln reply to yours I will explain things to you as well as I can. We have compiled a list of uaiformity of prices. for Warrington and all places surrounding here so that all the employers could compete in the same market. Oae master has been paying more than another which is not fair.O.lr Executive Committer] sent each employer of WarrinAton one of the lists and gave them a mouth's notice before it came into force, and al.sr: seven day]] to B,nd any alterations or sugge-itions. It shluld have come in force on the 6th May. At the expiration of that time .the masters wrote to our Executive Committee and a-kel for another seven days to consider things. This was granted thorn but since then the Executive Commit ]ree have 1,,d no word frau them in any shape or form hut they had been compiling a Pat to suit tneinselve.]. Some articles they have reduced and others have advanced, and we men mean to stand by the list which we have corn- pied and not the employers list—what we deserve for our labour. There are 42 of us out o strikb and the employers' names are as follows :—Mr Thomas Hutchin- son, Mr 11,13 by and Mr Danit Johnson. oir ]cutiye committee wrote to Mr Jahnsoe„the secretary of the em- ployers for an int.rview on Monday last, the 29-11 but th-y ignored tt,which we con.aiJer non:Emil,. Hoping you nil he able to see from the infonnati ,n I have given you thv iu (lance is jut, I beg to remaia youls in Unity, G</p>
        <p>, „ Mr BEMSINGTON the delegate' for the Union attended the men lag aid stated that the men considered that it was not fair for,one employer to go into the market and cut under another. They wanted uniformity of prices. ' The men who were employed at Mr J Ihneon's until recently received 8 I per hamper in winter, whieh was counted as 32 -weeks' and 9 tin summer which con-iseed of the other 20 weeks. Mr Johnson was approached by the Eirecut've Committee, and with the other emoloyers agree.i to piy the men 811 during the winter. He con- tinued to pay that am runt until last Saturday week and then he offrrred to give an adv ince of Al for the ermsieg twenty weeks and then reduce it to 8 I again in the winter. With regard to the hours of work some men commenced at six o'clock in the mornieg and continued mot seven or eight in the evening, atic then received 4e 6$ or Siwages. They bad barely time to get their masts. It was nothing better than slavery. They presented a list to the employers and the em- ployers pre.eneed a hit to them: In the latter list there was a difference of 11 on one article and if on another. With regard to the wages the men de,ie,el as much in winter as in automat% Before they would accep the employers' list the men would leave the t iwn. Another grievance was that they had to pay for their own light and for the preparation of the willows. It cost them 2i or 31 per week for light and 81 or 91 for cleaning the willows. The men had asked for the willows to be prepared fir then and the light to be provided free. Mr Harriman There is no Arm is town psyiag what you le Tura ? Mr BEBBINGTON N. They are paying that much in tiverroor end Mawdeley.</p>
        <p>Mr Wtm.cocas : la it the rule of the trade that you pay for willows to be prepared ? Mr BEHBINGTON This is the only place in Lancas- shire aril Coeshire where they pay for th'na. In answer to a further question Mr B ,bhington ssid thst the men worked on an average firm 121 to 13 Wilts ner day and received about 41t I or 5d per nour. Mr Wittcoacq : It IR not enougti. • The CHAIRMAN AO I 13, thiught they ought to try and bring the C mediation 134ard in action in the strike if possiblA.lt seemed to be a case In which the Conciliation Board should fix a standard rate of wages in the dig trict. He thought that if a letter went from the B lard to the executive of the Skip and Baskot Maker',llnion and also t the employers it would help to bring about a sprVernent. When ihs Board met again he vr+ulci ae It something could not bs done to put an end to • the strike.</p>
        <p>Mr PLitcsvots sugzested that if the men found they could not come to terms with the masters they should @west to the employers that the case be referred to th-Concilaton fl.tard. The matter then dropped, Mr BEBBINGTIN intimating that thu• be glad t-) receive any grants from societies desirous of he'ping them, The GERMAN said at a meeting of the 13 lard he mentioned rhe strike and also th strike ot cabinet malt.ra,•anci it was then stated by the President of the Bo.rd that the services of the B .arri had been rif ,red to the employers. Tie employers said it was not a mittor for cuic nation as they did not think the strike would laqt long. TIM LOT OF SHOP AsikiTANTS —COUNCIL 913M,M1T Ma C.</p>
      </div>
