      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>CLUB. - -- Reports and Statement of Accounts.</p>
        <p>ThA memb^rs of the Warrington Football Club bay, thia week bee., Waited in possession of the annual re- ports and atatentect of ac ;omits for the pest season. they are t e most favourable reports which have been preaented fir many years. Mr Ashton hai a splendid account give of the team's performancea and Mr IPemberton is able to present a balance sheet, a/lowing the club to be worth acrriething appre.ching £l,OOO. the annual meeting will be field next Tuesday in the Workmen's Club, Marius,. Place, when the members will have an cop ottunity of discussing the reports and elect- ing the of 6 .ers for h+ ensuing year.</p>
        <p>MR HON. SRCRSTART'S REPORT. It is with teenage of Rustled atioo, says Mr H Ashton The hon secretary), that I am enabled to submit you A very favourable rep It of the pas seaerm'a work. The is' team have played 39 nseti hes, 23 being won, 12 lost, 4 ,Irawn The "A" team have p eyed 27 matches, won 16 It 7 and drawn 4. Efforts were made before the Commencement of the season to get the team in mond co-alition, with a desire for a promising start. starting with a victory over Widnes on their own ground, Salford were Pert accounted for, followed by a I drawn gem" with St Helens, a clear gain of 5 points on last seasoree results. Th n csme aucceasive defeus by Rowell on Rangers away araci S winton at home, the Blues in 'kill? winning 119 3 pows to nil, after a splendid match. A fine p 'outs against Rochdale Hornets was followed by the biggest defeat of the season by 14 points 'o3 at Barrow. The match against Oldham was a remark sten one from the fact that we finished the earn, with II men, and then only lost by 6 points to nil, a rneritsrious ptrformance under the circumstances. Roncorn on foil g Day was another ease of Very hard droncied goal only standing against us Varied succ, as will we rn..t and defeated Broughtnn Rangers at home in the re urn match ; then a iniz foluenne of y„,priane victories over Stockport.Leels.H ck.noodwike. Leeds Parish, 8.a,, fort, Maraningbem, Mission, Leigh and Ca-tleford. Toe final tie of the N. Ii Cup at Leeds unfortunately ended disastrnuely for us A drawn game with Leigh at Wigan on the Monday Wait following for the final of, the S W. Lancashire, Cup, brought the aeon to a close. .The team generally have performed r markably well through the season, particularly during the latter reirtinn, when they rec ode] 10 wins in sac- cartoon. The defence has been well maintained,and has shown a great improvement en previous seasons. The *lief/ witheut oouht has demonstrated the fact that it is one of the most dangerous in the County, and as the majority of the Ist team have youth on their side, with the judicious introduction of 'a good clever iialf-back and more weiarlst forward, they should most certainly maintain that form they displayed , during the latter portion of the season just concluded. ! Floe players have responded nobly to tne efforts and wishes of the c onmittee during the season to keep fit, end their discipline generally has been exemplary. R 'girding monetary matters the team have expressed , their entire saiisfaorion with the off its made to them, even up to and including the Batley mate"). Twenty. one new 'slayers have been signed on daring the season, and th- Fimior COmpetilion passed a ruling chewing e fee of 2, 6 I each, and also Confining each club to 75 players. The fee was intro -need to strengthen the funds of the compreition. J. Fish followed tbe trample sif Morriaen be securing the cnampionehip medal by virtue of Lancashire County winning the championship A movument is in progress regarding the formation of .a new league to he ea, el the N nth rn Rugby Football League be the firs' fit übs in each °twit By bating Leigh is” Easter lidnday and taking the 6.h place we received an invitation CO j Hu, and your com- p, ,tres in their desire to keep the club in the front rank decided to accept. Flo far a anborominittee hal, been formed to facilrate'matters, and the League will take practieable shape in the course of a few weeks. Several of our placers have already signed on for next season, including F.sh. To Mr Pemberton I must accord my indebtednessi for the assistance he has afforded me. Tee C Mesos Bowers, Dillon, Nevins, Barnes, Tnrortilley, and 11-ieritt, are'entitled to my thanks for their ponctinslity, attendance, and careful onn-ideration of matters con earning the Club. The in mberasad sup- porters generally are to be congratulated —not only by toe offl,!isla and the team—but by everyone having the I Cliah's w-lfare at hear ,for the apon'aneons and enthust- aetic seaport they accorded the players during the Cup. ties. The f act that we paid the Railway Companies something like £9OO tor fares only, moat convince toe moat ~c,,p, ie al or weir loyalty to the Club. To Leeds (Isr round) we had alien t 1.000 supporters (13 saloon n•eies); B ughten over 2,000, and to the tin ii at Leeds 4,000. siod 65 .akone. Tie ft ai way people are much to blimp for tee very bad facilities afforded our excur- sions to Leeds, They cootetid the daisy was caused by local ciongesticn of traffic between bludderafitld and Leeds.</p>
      </div>
