        <p>at Vietorie Mines, co:Raining 41:1013t 40 pee meat of nickel and 40 per a nt. of -opper. will b., trarisported to Clydach. near Swansea. In Wales. where extenaive worke for IP treatment caleu ated for an annual production of 1(0) to 1500 t Of nickel and 4000 to 6 00 toes of copper sulphate are in course of construction, under the diotcti in of Dr Carl Langer. who has been Dr Mond's collaborator in the working out of his process from the beginning. These works are expected to be completed and started in the Autumn of this year rha ',Mika have base designed CO as to render future extensten easy and eeoriensic I. The property at Clytaeh, on which th 3 wurks are being erected, has a lent frontage to th siwanma Val:ey Canal. giving easy rimless to the Part of -wansea ; and is connected by a siding to the Midland Railway Tie consumption of nickel is now about 91703 tuts per an rim, and is gr.wing rapidly mainly on account of trio expansion of the proiliwtioia ce nick I steel. which is tarter,- lively used in sh.p.buildi ig, partimlarly in the construction of warships, armour-plates. and for innurnerable other pur- poses where great strength and comparative lightness are of imoortance - for instance, in tha meiotic: ore of or inance, r fie barrels, propeller shafts. locomotive axles, bicscles. Art.; thus the output of th • Oompauy may, within a few years, be considerably increased Without 'drawling the market. The consnmption of; sulphate of copper—mainly for the prevention of a disease of the vine—is over 100 0)0 tons per annern, and is rapidly increasing, so that the limited gum., tity to be produced by the Company will easily be placed at full prices. Careful estimates made by the manage sof the works and endorsed by Dr. Mond show that at present prices the value of the sulpha, eof copper produced will about cover the total exeense of mining, smelt!, g. and relining the ore now being raised at the mines, leaving practically the value of the nictel as profit. 