        <p>THE VOLUNTEERS. --- High Honour for the Warrington Battalion. The Annual Encampment. A special battalion order was issued on Thursday last week by the Commanding Officer of the 114 V. 13. South Lancashire Regiment (P.W.V.) to the following affect:— " Orders have been reeeive4 to-day cancelling the encampment of the Battalion with the Lancashire Volunteer Infantry Brigade at Conway on the 26:111 /day. As soon as further information or orders are received they *ill be notified to the Battalion, which will In all probability now encamp at a considerably , later date. The earliest opportunity is now taken of , inning this order, so Lb, t members of the Corps may avoid obtaining leave to attend camp at Whitsuntide I and in those cases where this has already been done it I is hoped that employers will be favourably inclined to allow the men to go at a later date instead." On Friday evening the following announcement was issued :—" The Commanding Oflher has great pleasure in announcing to all ranks that the Battalion has been selected as one of the Special Volunteer Batlations forming a poriion of the New Army Corp about to be organised. This is a high lonoer both to the Battalion and to Warrington, which the commanding Officer is sure will be heartily appri ciated. The present good reputation of the Battalion ka solely been won by bird work in the poit, and can erly no maintained by hard work in the future on the part of every memter of thr Corps ; which the Commanding Officer is confident will he heartily and loyally given to keep the Warrington Battalion in tbe place it has won in the front rank of the volunteer force. The Battalion will now encamp with the Army Corps Volunteer Brigade to which it is affiliated, at some later date which will be notified as soon as °ulna on this point are received from superior authority."</p>
